window.COURSE_CATALOGUE_DATA = {"generatedFrom":"University of Auckland Course Catalogue","courses":[{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 701","title":"创建可维护的软件","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20701","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 701 - Creating Maintainable Software","prescription":"Developing maintainable software has been an ongoing challenge in the software industry. This course presents the principles and practices that have been proposed for developing maintainable software systems. It will evaluate and critique these principles and practices through examining their application in practice and through understanding the research on their effectiveness. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 331 or 718.","overview":"Software maintainability refers to how much effort is required to keep a software product useful. A challenge in the software industry is that the majority of the lifetime cost of software product comes after it is first deployed. The better the maintainability of the software the lower the future costs, so understanding how to develop maintainable software is crucial. This course provides an in-depth look at what it means for software to be maintainable. It presents the principles and practices that have been proposed for developing maintainable software. Students will learn to evaluate and critique these principles and practices by examining their application in practice and through understanding the research on their effectiveness. This will prepare students for developing high-quality software products in the industry. Students are expected to have prior experience in objected-oriented programming using Java.","topics":"Clean Code, Code quality, Code smells, Clean Architecture, Design quality, Design patterns, Antipatterns, Refactoring, SOLID principles, Comprehensibility, Alterability, Test-driven development, Testability","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect: 3 hours of lectures 3 hours of reading and thinking about the content 4 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation Students are also expected to have prior experience in objected-oriented programming using Java.","locations":"Semester Two 2026","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"软件可维护性是指需要付出多少努力才能保持软件产品的可用。软件行业面临的一个挑战是，软件产品的大部分生命周期成本发生在首次部署之后。软件的可维护性越好，未来的成本就越低，因此了解如何开发可维护的软件至关重要。本课程深入探讨软件可维护性的含义。它介绍了为开发可维护软件而提出的原则和实践。学生将通过检查其在实践中的应用并了解其有效性的研究来学习评估和批评这些原则和实践。这将为学生开发行业内的高质量软件产品做好准备。学生应具备使用 Java 进行面向对象编程的经验。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 702","title":"智能设备安全","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20702","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 702 - Security for Smart-devices","prescription":"Covers security features supported by the different platforms for smart devices. Provides an overview of the most popular OS platforms in the market and focuses on security for Android and iOS. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 340.","overview":"This course explores security features across major smart device platforms and provides an overview of the widely used OS platforms in the market. The course is structured in two parts. The first part delves into essential reverse engineering techniques (e.g., code analysis, dynamic and static analysis, de-obfuscation, and firmware exploitation) particularly within the context of Android. The second part is a group project, where students form teams to (i) develop and secure Android apps, (ii) reverse engineer Android apps created and secured by other teams, and (iii) present their findings on the security measures implemented in their own app and their approach to reverse engineering other teams' apps. Students are recommended to have a solid understanding of the following knowledge prior to enrolling: Linux operating systems, C programming, system architecture, and network programming.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 36 hours of lectures including seminar and project presentations. It is a research-oriented course.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程探讨主要智能设备平台的安全功能，并概述市场上广泛使用的操作系统平台。本课程分为两部分。第一部分深入研究基本的逆向工程技术（例如，代码分析、动态和静态分析、反混淆和固件利用），特别是在 Android 环境中。第二部分是一个小组项目，学生组成团队来 (i) 开发和保护 Android 应用程序，(ii) 对其他团队创建和保护的 Android 应用程序进行逆向工程，以及 (iii) 展示他们在自己的应用程序中实施的安全措施的发现以及对其他团队的应用程序进行逆向工程的方法。建议学生在入学前对以下知识有充分的了解：Linux操作系统、C语言编程、系统架构、网络编程。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 703","title":"人工智能泛化","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20703","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 703 - Generalising Artificial Intelligence","prescription":"AI deep learning has significantly advanced image understanding, language modelling, speech recognition, game playing, and more. These developments enable near-human capabilities in text and image generation. Explores highly specialised knowledge in planning, reasoning, explanation, natural language understanding, and knowledge acquisition, and assess their contribution to highly competent, general AI systems. Includes a significant individual research project.","overview":"The course aims to provide students with a comprehensive understanding of the key concepts related to Artificial General Intelligence (AGI), also known as general AI or advanced machine intelligence. Students are expected to describe and explain the fundamentals of general AI, including the components that distinguish it from task-specific AI, such as reasoning, memory/knowledge representation, and planning. They will also demonstrate specialized knowledge in the field by critically assessing various approaches to AGI. Students will be tasked with familiarising themselves with existing solutions to the challenges associated with AGI. They should be capable of implementing these solutions and/or extending aspects of them to achieve certain AGI properties. Proficiency in programming is essential for this course.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 points course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 36 hours of lectures, 48 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 66 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"该课程旨在让学生全面了解与通用人工智能（AGI）相关的关键概念，也称为通用人工智能或高级机器智能。学生需要描述和解释通用人工智能的基础知识，包括区分其与特定任务人工智能的组成部分，例如推理、记忆/知识表示和规划。他们还将通过批判性评估各种通用人工智能方法来展示该领域的专业知识。学生的任务是熟悉针对 AGI 相关挑战的现有解决方案。他们应该能够实施这些解决方案和/或扩展它们的各个方面以实现某些 AGI 属性。熟练掌握编程对于本课程至关重要。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 704","title":"人机交互基础","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20704","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 704 - Fundamentals in Human-Computer Interaction","prescription":"Human behaviour and humans’ expectations of interaction. Computer interfaces and the interaction between humans and computers. The significance of the user interface, interface design and user-centred design process in system development. Interface usability evaluation methodologies and practice. Includes processes of evaluation, design and implementation using current techniques and tools.","overview":"Welcome to Human Computer Interaction (HCI). For computer users, the interface 'is' the system and for the 'average' system over half the code is entirely about managing the user interaction. This makes HCI an important area of study and research within Computer Science. As you will find during the course, HCI encompasses everything from designing a simple web page like this to developing Virtual Reality environments! We often combine knowledge from psychology, design and computer science. The course emphasises two complementary competencies of HCI: the ability to evaluate the usability of a user interface, and the ability to design a usable interface (e.g., through User Experience - UX - Design), and these will be part of the coursework. We aim to have guest lecturers in the course to offer broader perspectives.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, each week you can expect: 2 hours of lectures A 2-hour tutorial 2 hours of reading and thinking about the content 4 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"欢迎来到人机交互 (HCI)。对于计算机用户来说，界面“就是”系统，而对于“普通”系统来说，超过一半的代码完全是关于管理用户交互的。这使得人机交互成为计算机科学领域的重要学习和研究领域。正如您在课程中会发现的那样，HCI 涵盖了从设计这样的简单网页到开发虚拟现实环境的一切！我们经常结合心理学、设计和计算机科学的知识。该课程强调人机交互​​的两个互补能力：评估用户界面可用性的能力，以及设计可用界面的能力（例如，通过用户体验 - 用户体验 - 设计），这些将成为课程的一部分。我们的目标是在课程中聘请客座讲师，以提供更广阔的视角。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 705","title":"人机交互高级主题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20705","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 705 - Advanced Topics in Human Computer Interaction","prescription":"Human aspects of computer systems, relevant to commercial solution development and computer science research. Sample topics: advanced evaluation methods; support of pen and touch-based interaction; trends with domain-specific user interface design, such as interfaces for enterprise systems.","overview":"This course focuses on recent research in Human Computer Interaction, covering a broad variety of topic areas and domains. As HCI is involved in all end-user software, there are myriad software applications that relate to HCI. In this course, we focus on research methods, critically evaluating research involving human computer interaction, and synthesizing results from published research. Several guest lectures will discuss the applications of HCI in their own domains and research areas.Another component of this course is the research project proposal, which will be conducted in teams. In the first half of the semester, you will carry out a literature review related to that topic. In parallel with that, and with more intensity in the second half of the semester, you will work with your team to propose a research project, including the design and implementation of software as well as data collection. The skills developed in this course are particularly useful for those wishing to have a career involving UX/UI (user experience/user interaction/user interfaces) and front-end development.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect: 3 hours of lectures 3 hours of reading and thinking about the content 4 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程重点关注人机交互的最新研究，涵盖广泛的主题领域和领域。由于HCI涉及所有最终用户软件，因此与HCI相关的软件应用有无数。在本课程中，我们重点关注研究方法，批判性地评估涉及人机交互的研究，并综合已发表的研究结果。几位客座讲座将讨论人机交互在各自领域和研究领域的应用。本课程的另一个组成部分是研究项目提案，该提案将以团队形式进行。在本学期的前半部分，您将进行与该主题相关的文献综述。与此同时，在本学期的后半段，您将与您的团队合作提出一个研究项目，包括软件的设计和实施以及数据收集。本课程中培养的技能对于那些希望从事 UX/UI（用户体验/用户交互/用户界面）和前端开发职业的人特别有用。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 706","title":"专题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20706","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 706 - Special Topic","prescription":"An exploration of emerging or niche areas in science that are not covered in the core curriculum.","overview":"This course is designed for students keen to explore emerging or niche areas in science. Learning outcomes include advanced knowledge in a specialised subject area, critically evaluating current research and practice, and communicating scientific ideas in written reports and/or oral presentations. No special preparation is required though a BSc-level knowledge of science and general interest in the special topic is assumed.","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程专为热衷于探索科学新兴或利基领域的学生而设计。学习成果包括专业学科领域的先进知识、批判性地评估当前的研究和实践，以及在书面报告和/或口头演示中传达科学思想。尽管假设具有理学士学位水平的科学知识并且对特定主题具有普遍兴趣，但不需要特殊准备。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 707","title":"专题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20707","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 707 - Special Topic","prescription":"An exploration of emerging or niche areas in science that are not covered in the core curriculum.","overview":"This course is designed for students keen to explore emerging or niche areas in science. Learning outcomes include advanced knowledge in a specialised subject area, critically evaluating current research and practice, and communicating scientific ideas in written reports and/or oral presentations. No special preparation is required though a BSc-level knowledge of science and general interest in the special topic is assumed.","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程专为热衷于探索科学新兴或利基领域的学生而设计。学习成果包括专业学科领域的先进知识、批判性地评估当前的研究和实践，以及在书面报告和/或口头演示中传达科学思想。尽管假设具有理学士学位水平的科学知识并且对特定主题具有普遍兴趣，但不需要特殊准备。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 710","title":"导向学习","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20710","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 710 - Directed Study","prescription":"A self-directed course for students embarking on a guided research project, dissertation or thesis to develop and apply scientific skills and competencies under the supervision of one or more academic staff members.","overview":"A research-focused course emphasising self-directed work to support the development and application of key academic competencies and scientific research skills. Assessment methods may include peer review, poster or oral presentations, research proposals, and written reports or a thesis.","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"以研究为重点的课程，强调自主工作，以支持关键学术能力和科学研究技能的发展和应用。评估方法可能包括同行评审、海报或口头演示、研究计划以及书面报告或论文。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 711","title":"并行与分布式计算","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20711","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 711 - Parallel and Distributed Computing","prescription":"Computer architectures and languages for exploring parallelism, conceptual models of parallelism, principles for programming in a parallel environment, different models to achieve interprocess communication, concurrency control, distributed algorithms and fault tolerance. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 320 or 335.","overview":"This course focuses on algorithms and techniques for developing parallel and distributed applications and their architectures and systems. Parallel computing on a single computer uses multiple processors to process tasks in parallel, whereas distributed parallel computing uses multiple computing devices to process those tasks. In this course, students will explore distributed consensus, the CAP theorem, logical time, multicast, mutex, garbage collection, snapshots, deadlock detection, web caching, resource looking up in the P2P system, blockchain, multiprocessor scheduling, and multiprocessor caching.","topics":"Distributed searches Distributed consensus The CAP theorem Logical time Distributed snapshots Mutual exclusion Deadlock detection Distributed garbage collection Scheduling problems","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect: 3 hours of lectures 4 hours of reading and thinking about the content 3 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程重点介绍用于开发并行和分布式应用程序及其架构和系统的算法和技术。单台计算机上的并行计算使用多个处理器并行处理任务，而分布式并行计算使用多个计算设备来处理这些任务。在本课程中，学生将探索分布式共识、CAP 定理、逻辑时间、多播、互斥、垃圾收集、快照、死锁检测、Web 缓存、P2P 系统中的资源查找、区块链、多处理器调度和多处理器缓存。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 715","title":"高级计算机图形学","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20715","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 715 - Advanced Computer Graphics","prescription":"An advanced look at current research issues in computer graphics. Typical topics include: ray-tracing acceleration methods; radiosity; subdivision surfaces; physically-based modelling; animation; image-based lighting and rendering; non-photorealistic rendering; advanced texturing. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 373.","overview":"COMPSCI 715 Advanced Computer Graphics is designed to train students to develop cutting-edge computer graphics and AR/VR technologies and to evaluate them and communicate results. The course is most suitable for students interested in novel computer graphics algorithms and applications and who consider a career involving developing computer graphics and virtual reality software and/or doing research in this field. Knowledge of introductory computer graphics (e.g. COMPSCI 373) is an advantage but is not required as long as students are willing to self-learn the required basic concepts. The course will include an advanced look at current research issues in computer graphics. Typical topics are listed below, although the precise content may vary from year to year. The course comprises three key components: A project implementing and analysing a solution to a computer graphics research problem. A report describing the investigated research problem, its solution and its evaluation. Lectures teaching research skills and selected topics relevant to the projects.","topics":"The following list contains typical topics taught in CS715. The exact list of topics depends on selected group projects and usually only a subset of the topics below is taught. Virtual and augmented reality Serious games Procedural modelling Visualisation Ray-tracing acceleration methods Radiosity Subdivision surfaces Physically-based modelling Animation Image-based lighting and rendering Non-photorealistic rendering Advanced texturing","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, each week you can expect: 3 hours of lectures, presentations and team meetings 1-2 hours of reading and thinking about the content 5-6 hours of work on writing source code and reports","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"COMPSCI 715 高级计算机图形学旨在培训学生开发尖端计算机图形学和 AR/VR 技术并对其进行评估和交流结果。该课程最适合对新颖的计算机图形算法和应用感兴趣并考虑从事开发计算机图形和虚拟现实软件和/或在该领域进行研究的职业的学生。计算机图形学入门知识（例如 COMPSCI 373）是一个优势，但只要学生愿意自学所需的基本概念，就不是必需的。该课程将包括对计算机图形学当前研究问题的深入了解。下面列出了典型主题，但具体内容可能每年有所不同。该课程包括三个关键组成部分： 实施和分析计算机图形研究问题的解决方案的项目。描述所调查的研究问题、解决方案和评估的报告。讲座教授研究技能和与项目相关的选定主题。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 720","title":"高级算法设计与分析","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20720","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 720 - Advanced Design and Analysis of Algorithms","prescription":"Selected advanced topics in design and analysis of algorithms, such as: combinatorial enumeration algorithms; advanced graph algorithms; analytic and probabilistic methods in the analysis of algorithms; randomised algorithms; methods for attacking NP-hard problems.","overview":"Algorithm design and analysis is a fundamental and important part of computer science. This course introduces students to advanced techniques for the design and analysis of algorithms and explores some applications of the resulting algorithms. The first part of this course studies advanced algorithms for families of graphs of bounded combinatorial width (treewidth, pathwidth, treedepth, branchwidth), often formulated as linear-time dynamic programs, for many popular NP-hard problems. The second part covers parameterised and approximation algorithms for several classical NP-hard optimisation problems. If time allows, we will also look at applications from computational biology. No knowledge of biology is required! The material taught in this course is particularly relevant for all students wishing to pursue further postgraduate studies in theoretical computer science or discrete mathematics and for those who are planning for a career that involves efficient programming and/or problem-solving.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 3 hours of lectures, 3 hours of reading and thinking about the content, and 4 hours of work on assignments and other coursework.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 320","overviewZh":"算法设计和分析是计算机科学的基础和重要组成部分。本课程向学生介绍算法设计和分析的先进技术，并探讨所得算法的一些应用。本课程的第一部分研究有界组合宽度（树宽、路径宽度、树深度、分支宽度）图族的高级算法，通常将其表述为线性时间动态程序，适用于许多流行的 NP 难题。第二部分涵盖了几个经典 NP 难优化问题的参数化和近似算法。如果时间允许，我们还将研究计算生物学的应用。无需生物学知识！本课程教授的材料对于所有希望在理论计算机科学或离散数学领域继续深造的学生以及那些正在规划涉及高效编程和/或解决问题的职业的学生特别重要。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 721","title":"随机算法与概率方法","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20721","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 721 - Randomised Algorithms and Probabilistic Methods","prescription":"Randomised algorithms are algorithms that “flip coins” to make decisions. In many cases, such algorithms are faster, simpler, or more elegant than the classical, deterministic ones. Covers basic principles and techniques used to design and analyse randomised algorithms, and applications of randomised methods in mathematics and computer science. Recommended preparation: STATS 125, COMPSCI 225 or MATHS 254, COMPSCI 320.","overview":"Randomness is at core of many areas of computer science, from complexity theory and design of efficient algorithms to distributed computing and machine learning. In this course we learn the basics of probabilistic analysis by studying two closely related topics: randomised algorithms and probabilistic method. Use of a “coin flip” to make decisions makes it possible for algorithms to avoid worst-case scenarios. Consequently, if one is willing to accept a small probability of an error, such randomised algorithms are often faster, simpler, or simply more elegant than deterministic ones. Examples include Welzl’s algorithm for finding a smallest enclosing ball, Karger’s min-cut algorithm, Luby’s algorithm for maximal independent set in a distributed setting, and colour-coding paradigm for finding long paths. Rather than being solely a concept of study of probability theory, randomness has also become a core tool in many other areas of mathematics. Use of “coin flips” to construct graphs with complex properties or to unveil patterns constitutes a technique known as the probabilistic method. We cover examples such as constructions of graphs with large girth and large chromatic number, and constructions of the so-called expander graphs, one of the central objects in theoretical computer science. We aim to cover a range of tools and techniques used in probabilistic analysis through study of randomised algorithms, applications of linearity of expectation in analysis of randomised algorithms and probabilistic method, basic concentration inequalities such as Markov’s, Chebyshev’s, and Chernoff’s inequality, as well as more advanced ones such as inequalities of Azuma and Talagrand, analysis of hashing through balls-into-bins problem, compression argument used in analysis of Moser’s LLL algorithm, random walks on graphs and Markov chains, combinatorial properties of random structures and probabilistic construction of expanders, applications of expanders in theoretical computer science, and so on. This is a fast paced theoretical course with no programming assignments! The emphasis is on mathematically rigorous analysis and proofs. Previous experience in probability and discrete mathematics is necessary. Experience in design of algorithms is recommended. To help you decide whether this course would be interesting for you, consider the following two problems: 1. Design efficient (randomised) algorithm which finds a path of length log(n) in a graph G. 2. Construct a graph with n vertices such that there is no subset of vertices of size log(n)/2 with the following property: either all vertices are connected by an edge, or none is. In both problems, flipping a coin is crucial! Don’t worry if you do not manage to solve them – these are difficult problems with surprisingly elegant solutions that we cover in the first week.","topics":"Randomised algorithms Concentration inequalities and tail bounds for discrete random variables Probabilistic method Random walks and Markov chains Expander graphs","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 3 hours of lectures, 2 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 5 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MSc - Master of Science","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"随机性是计算机科学许多领域的核心，从复杂性理论和高效算法的设计到分布式计算和机器学习。在本课程中，我们通过研究两个密切相关的主题来学习概率分析的基础知识：随机算法和概率方法。使用“抛硬币”来做出决策使得算法可以避免最坏的情况。因此，如果人们愿意接受很小的错误概率，那么这种随机算法通常比确定性算法更快、更简单，或者更优雅。示例包括用于查找最小包围球的 Welzl 算法、Karger 的最小割算法、用于分布式设置中最大独立集的 Luby 算法以及用于查找长路径的颜色编码范例。随机性不仅仅是概率论研究的一个概念，它还成为许多其他数学领域的核心工具。使用“抛硬币”来构建具有复杂属性的图或揭示模式构成了一种称为概率方法的技术。我们介绍了一些示例，例如具有大周长和大色数的图的构造，以及所谓的扩展图的构造，这是理论计算机科学的中心对象之一。我们的目标是通过研究随机算法、期望线性在随机算法和概率方法分析中的应用、马尔科夫不等式、切比雪夫不等式和切尔诺夫不等式等基本集中不等式，以及 Azuma 和 Talagrand 不等式等更高级的不等式、通过球入箱问题进行哈希分析、Moser 分析中使用的压缩论证来涵盖概率分析中使用的一系列工具和技术。 LLL算法、图和马尔可夫链上的随机游走、随机结构的组合特性和扩展器的概率构造、扩展器在理论计算机科学中的应用等等。这是一门快节奏的理论课程，没有编程作业！重点是数学上严格的分析和证明。之前有概率和离散数学经验是必要的。推荐有算法设计经验。为了帮助您确定本课程是否对您感兴趣，请考虑以下两个问题： 1. 设计高效（随机）算法，在图 G 中查找长度为 log(n) 的路径。 2. 构造一个具有 n 个顶点的图，使得不存在具有以下属性的大小为 log(n)/2 的顶点子集：要么所有顶点都由边连接，要么没有。在这两个问题中，抛硬币至关重要！如果您无法解决这些问题，请不要担心 - 这些都是难题，我们将在第一周介绍令人惊讶的优雅解决方案。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 725","title":"可用安全与隐私工程","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20725","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 725 - Usable Security and Privacy Engineering","prescription":"The human aspect of cybersecurity and privacy engineering is relevant to commercial solution development and cybersecurity and privacy research. Sample topics: secure systems design; usable security systems evaluation; privacy-preserving software systems; threat modelling; economics of usable security and privacy; OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities. Recommended preparation: 30 points from COMPSCI 313, 314, 320, 335, 340, 351, 702, 734, 742.","overview":"Computer security has become increasingly crucial with the rise of e-commerce, the computerisation of workplaces, and the availability of e-government services. Systems are vulnerable to various attacks, such as denial of service, unauthorised modifications, and misuse. This course provides a real-world perspective on computer security, aiming for systems that are affordable, usable, and trustworthy, rather than “perfect security.” We will explore recent technical literature on specific computer security topics, including forensic computing, secure systems design tradeoffs, mis-identification threats and defences, ethical security, human factors in security, and software security techniques and vulnerabilities.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 3 hours of lectures per week, a 1 hour tutorial in the week prior to your scheduled oral presentation (i.e. approx. 0.1 hours per week, on average), 2 hours per week of reading and thinking about the content and 3.9 hours of work per week on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"随着电子商务的兴起、工作场所的计算机化以及电子政务服务的可用性，计算机安全变得越来越重要。系统容易受到各种攻击，例如拒绝服务、未经授权的修改和滥用。本课程提供了计算机安全的现实视角，旨在打造经济实惠、可用且值得信赖的系统，而不是“完美的安全性”。我们将探讨有关特定计算机安全主题的最新技术文献，包括取证计算、安全系统设计权衡、错误识别威胁和防御、道德安全、安全中的人为因素以及软件安全技术和漏洞。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 726","title":"网络防御与对策","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20726","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 726 - Network Defence and Countermeasures","prescription":"Focuses on the use and deployment of protective systems used in securing internal and external networks. Examines in detail the widely used protocols, including SSL, IPSec and DNSSEC, as well as covers infrastructure platform protocols including wireless security (IEEE 802.11). Explores current research and developments in the area of network defence and countermeasures. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 314, 315.","overview":"This course focuses on protective systems used in securing internal and external networks. It covers a range of security technologies such as firewalls, NAT, IDS, honeypot, and a host of other security mechanisms for providing security at different TCP/IP layers. It examines in detail the widely used protocols including SSL, IPSec, and DNSSEC as well as covers topics related to securing the infrastructure including wireless security (IEEE 802.11). This course also explores the current research and developments in the area of network defence and countermeasures. The research aspect is mainly covered in individual seminars, which are based on recent research articles published at top-notch venues. There is a group report, which typically extends the presented articles. Finally, there is a final exam based on lectures and seminars. The skills developed in this course are particularly useful for those wishing to have a career involving cybersecurity. The course project will be helpful for those who are planning to develop expertise in writing research reports.","topics":"Cryptography and PKI OSI and TCP/IP Models SSL/TLS DNS and DNSSEC DoS and DDoS Firewall IDS and Honeypot Wifi Security IPv4, IPv6, and IPSec BGP and SDN","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, each week you can expect: 3 hours of lectures 3-4 hours for group projects each week 4-5 hours of reading and thinking about the content As well as, approximately 20 hours for individual seminars across the semester.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MProfStuds - Master of Professional Studies","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程重点介绍用于保护内部和外部网络的保护系统。它涵盖了一系列安全技术，例如防火墙、NAT、IDS、蜜罐以及许多其他安全机制，用于在不同的 TCP/IP 层提供安全性。它详细研究了广泛使用的协议，包括 SSL、IPSec 和 DNSSEC，并涵盖了与保护基础设施相关的主题，包括无线安全 (IEEE 802.11)。本课程还探讨了网络防御和对策领域的当前研究和发展。研究方面主要以个人研讨会的形式进行，这些研讨会基于最近在顶级场所发表的研究文章。有一个小组报告，通常会扩展所呈现的文章。最后，还有基于讲座和研讨会的期末考试。本课程中培养的技能对于那些希望从事网络安全职业的人特别有用。该课程项目将对那些计划培养撰写研究报告专业知识的人有所帮助。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 727","title":"密码管理","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20727","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 727 - Cryptographic Management - Level 9","prescription":"Builds on best practices, and compliance standards to establish an advanced understanding of modern cryptographic systems used in securing communications and data storage. Advanced knowledge in modern cryptography management issues such as algorithm selection, generation, distribution, and revocation of encryption keys are applied through a research-based report and a group project. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 210 or MATHS 120","overview":"The course introduces the building blocks of cryptography and the use and deployment of cryptographic systems used in securing communications and data storage. It provides an overview of encryption algorithms and the technologies and systems that support them including symmetric key cryptography, public key infrastructure, digital signatures, and certificate technologies. As a part of this overview, this course will concentrate on the management issues of algorithm selection; encryption key generation, distribution, and revocation; and strategies for overcoming encryption security vulnerabilities and their associated breaches. The course also discusses best practices and compliance with national and international standards and explores the current research and developments in this area.","topics":"Key topics that will be covered include: Symmetric Encryption - block and stream ciphers, modes of operation, hashing Asymmetric Encryption - RSA, Elgamal, Diffie-Hellman, Ellptic curves, PKI, Digital Signatures Security and attack models and countermeasures - IND-CCA Key Management and Distribution Crypto-systems This is a self-contained course in Applied Cryptography and all important technical ideas are explained in the lecture notes and during lectures. In particular, the lectures in the first weeks contain an overview of some basic mathematical concepts such as modular arithmetic and simple probability. However, the following general knowledge is assumed (typically covered in the first and second year of a computer science degree): bits, bytes, words, basic logic operations (and, or, not) basic algebra, laws of exponents, primes, functions algorithms, complexity computer programs, compilation, executables data structures, arrays, computer memory, cache operating systems networks","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 3 hours of lectures, 3 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 4 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MProfStuds - Master of Professional Studies","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程介绍密码学的构建模块以及用于保护通信和数据存储的密码系统的使用和部署。它概述了加密算法以及支持它们的技术和系统，包括对称密钥加密、公钥基础设施、数字签名和证书技术。作为概述的一部分，本课程将集中讨论算法选择的管理问题；加密密钥生成、分发和撤销；以及克服加密安全漏洞及其相关漏洞的策略。该课程还讨论最佳实践和遵守国家和国际标准，并探讨该领域当前的研究和发展。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 732","title":"软件工具与技术","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20732","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 732 - Software Tools and Techniques","prescription":"An advanced course examining research issues related to tools and techniques for software design and development. Topics include: techniques for data mapping and data integration, software architectures for developing software tools, issues in advanced database systems. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 331 or SOFTENG 325 or COMPSCI 718 and 719","overview":"State-of-the-art software development, particularly in teams, requires the use of advanced tools to deliver high-quality software across the many platforms that we encounter today. It is characterized by a wide variety of techniques ranging from formal to informal, from automated to manual operations and covering programs as well as data. In this course, you'll leverage some of the latest full-stack development tools & frameworks such as MongoDB, React, NodeJS / Express, GitHub Actions, and Docker, to create a novel large-scale web application with a team of your classmates. You'll have almost full creative control over the direction of your project, which we will expect you to manage using software development best-practices. You'll also have the opportunity to research the latest developments in software tools & techniques and deliver a tutorial video explaining your research, and how others can get started using the tool of your choice.","topics":"MERN stack (MongoDB, Express, React, Node), GitHub actions, Docker","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in.","locations":"Semester One 2026","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"最先进的软件开发，特别是在团队中，需要使用先进的工具来跨我们今天遇到的许多平台交付高质量的软件。它的特点是技术种类繁多，从正式到非正式，从自动化到手工操作，涵盖程序和数据。在本课程中，您将利用一些最新的全栈开发工具和框架，例如 MongoDB、React、NodeJS / Express、GitHub Actions 和 Docker，与同学团队一起创建一个新颖的大型 Web 应用程序。您将对项目的方向拥有几乎完全的创造性控制，我们希望您使用软件开发最佳实践来管理项目的方向。您还将有机会研究软件工具和技术的最新发展，并提供教程视频来解释您的研究以及其他人如何开始使用您选择的工具。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 734","title":"网页、移动与企业计算","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20734","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 734 - Web, Mobile and Enterprise Computing","prescription":"Examines advanced and emerging software architectures at the confluence of XML, web services, distributed systems, and databases. Includes advanced topics in areas such as: mobile computing, remoting, web services for enterprise integration, workflow orchestrations for the enterprise, peer-to-peer computing, grid computing. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 335 or 718","overview":"In this course, you'll get exposure to the latest industry practices in cross-platform development. We'll explore how to create mobile-first user interfaces which are consistent across a wide variety of devices, and achieve rich functionality supported by cloud infrastructure & microservices. You'll learn how to develop, test and deploy these applications automatically with a CI/CD pipeline. You will learn to use AI effectively and responsibly to support your development. The course will be assessed via a range of individual and group assessments, including a team-based final project.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"在本课程中，您将了解跨平台开发的最新行业实践。我们将探索如何创建在各种设备上保持一致的移动优先用户界面，并实现云基础设施和微服务支持的丰富功能。您将学习如何使用 CI/CD 管道自动开发、测试和部署这些应用程序。您将学习有效且负责任地使用人工智能来支持您的发展。该课程将通过一系列个人和小组评估进行评估，包括基于团队的最终项目。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 742","title":"高级互联网：全球数据通信","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20742","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 742 - Advanced Internet: Global Data Communications","prescription":"The course covers wide area networks, global routing, network and protocol performance, buffering and queuing, advanced network measurement, network application performance, content networks, and advanced networking concepts. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 314 or 315","overview":"This is a research-oriented course covering various aspects of networked systems with a strong focus on the Internet and mobile network infrastructure. The course prepares students for roles in which they need to design and work with network infrastructure at the provider and carrier levels. Students who have completed this course will also be ready to undertake research in the general area of computer networking and distributed systems, including mobile and satellite systems. The course will be delivered mostly in the form of lectures, but will also include self-directed learning in the form of team projects and/or presentations.","topics":"Advanced concepts in TCP/IP Non-blocking and raw sockets Host- and network-based congestion control Bufferbloat Internet over satellite links Wireless Link budgets Cellular systems Network coding Global routing The Internet name and numbering system Software-defined networking Network simulation Multicasting Smart cities","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 150 hours in total for each 15 point course they are enrolled in. This equates to approximately 10 hours per week. For this course, each week you can expect 2-3 hours of lectures, 3 hours of reading and thinking about the content, and 4-5 hours of work on assignments and projects.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"这是一门研究型课程，涵盖网络系统的各个方面，重点关注互联网和移动网络基础设施。该课程帮助学生为需要在提供商和运营商级别设计和使用网络基础设施的角色做好准备。完成本课程的学生还将准备好在计算机网络和分布式系统（包括移动和卫星系统）的一般领域进行研究。该课程主要以讲座的形式进行，但也包括以团队项目和/或演示形式进行的自主学习。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 747","title":"计算教育","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20747","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 747 - Computing Education","prescription":"An overview of topics related to the use of technology in education and how people learn computer science concepts. Topics include research methodologies used in computer science education, how novices learn to program, and how technology can engage students in active learning, facilitate collaboration and enhance traditional educational practice. Recommended preparation: 30 points at Stage III in Computer Science or COMPSCI 718","overview":"This course has two major components - how students learn Computer Science (Computer Science Education) and how we can collect and use data from software systems to better engage students in learning (Computers in Education). The course assumes no prior background in education and would be of interest to students who want to know more about research on educational software tools or improve the learning of computing concepts. By the end of the course, students should be familiar with computing education research and be well prepared to engage in more substantial research projects in this area.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 150 hours in total for this course. This is approximately 12 hours per week over a 12-week period (including the study break). For this course, you can expect: 3 hours of in-class learning 4-5 hours of reading and thinking about content 6 hours of work on assignments","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"MProfStuds - Master of Professional Studies","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程有两个主要组成部分 - 学生如何学习计算机科学（计算机科学教育）以及我们如何收集和使用软件系统中的数据以更好地吸引学生参与学习（计算机教育）。该课程假设没有教育背景，并且想要了解更多有关教育软件工具的研究或提高计算概念学习的学生会感兴趣。在课程结束时，学生应该熟悉计算机教育研究，并为参与该领域更实质性的研究项目做好充分准备。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 750","title":"计算复杂性","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20750","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 750 - Computational Complexity","prescription":"Definitions of computational models and complexity classes: time complexity (e.g., P and NP), space complexity (e.g., L and PSPACE), circuit and parallel complexity (NC), polynomial-time hierarchy (PH), interactive complexity (IP), probabilistic complexity (BPP), and fixed-parameter complexity. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 320 or 350.","overview":"The course surveys classical and recent computational complexity results. Depending on the lecturers, the course will include descriptive string complexity, quantum computing, logic, and randomness.","topics":"Computational resources Logic, in particular satisfiability of Boolean formulas Classical versus quantum computing (depending on lecturer) Special topics, such as randomness, proof complexity, and others","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course. Students are expected to spend at least 10 hours per semester week involved in each 15 point course. For this course, each week you can expect 2 hours of contact hours, 4 hours of assignment preparation, and 4 hours of self-directed learning. These are only guidelines and extra study may be required if you missing knowledge of some areas.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"该课程调查了经典和最近的计算复杂性结果。根据讲师的不同，课程将包括描述性字符串复杂性、量子计算、逻辑和随机性。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 751","title":"数据库系统高级主题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20751","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 751 - Advanced Topics in Database Systems","prescription":"Database principles. Relational model, relational algebra, relational calculus, SQL, SQL and programming languages, entity-relationship model, normalisation, query processing and query optimisation, ACID transactions, transaction isolation levels, database recovery, database security, databases and XML. Research frontiers in database systems. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 220, 225 or COMPSCI 718.","overview":"Databases are at the core of most modern software applications. In this course you will learn the foundations of database systems, enabling you to build effective and efficient database applications. The course is divided into two parts: the first teaches you how to design and use a database application and the second covers transaction and looks in-depth at how database systems are built to work in the current changing environment.","topics":"Database Systems and Concepts Data Modeling – Entity-Relationship (ER) diagrams Relational Data Model and Database Design Structured Query Language (SQL) Relational Algebra, Functional Dependencies and Normalization Transaction processing Storage and Retrieval Data Models and Query Languages Distributed Data","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect: 3 hours of lectures A 1-hour tutorial 4 hours of reading and thinking about the content 2 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"数据库是大多数现代软件应用程序的核心。在本课程中，您将学习数据库系统的基础知识，使您能够构建有效且高效的数据库应用程序。本课程分为两部分：第一部分教您如何设计和使用数据库应用程序，第二部分介绍事务并深入研究如何构建数据库系统以在当前不断变化的环境中工作。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 752","title":"大数据管理","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20752","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 752 - Big Data Management","prescription":"The deep diversity of modern-day data from many companies requires data scientists to master many technologies that rely on new principles to represent, describe, access, and analyse data. The course will provide insight into the rich landscape of big data modelling, management, and analysis in distributed and heterogeneous environments. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 220, 351","overview":"Many companies must manage large volumes of diverse data in order to stay competitive. The deep diversity of modern-day data requires data scientists to master many technologies that rely on new principles to represent, describe, and access data. The course will provide insight into the rich landscape of big data. The main aim of the course is to prepare students for big data modelling and large-scale data management in distributed and heterogeneous environments. On the one hand, learning the principles of big data management will prepare students for a career as data scientists, independently of continuous technology changes. In particular, learning how to model, query, and integrate big data are necessary skills to get data ready for analytical purposes. For example, MapReduce algorithms such as PageRank illustrate how to efficiently rank billions of Web pages. On the other hand, investigating current big data technologies will demonstrate what is and what is not possible today, but also highlight opportunities for future work. For instance, Spark offers an integrated technology framework for preparing and analyzing big data, while the disruptive Blockchain technology exemplifies a distributed computing system with high fault tolerance with application potential that we are only beginning to understand.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect: 3 hours of lectures 1 hour of tutorial for every topic 3 hours of reading and thinking about the content 3 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MProfStuds - Master of Professional Studies","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"许多公司必须管理大量不同的数据才能保持竞争力。现代数据的深度多样性要求数据科学家掌握许多依赖新原理来表示、描述和访问数据的技术。该课程将深入了解大数据的丰富景观。该课程的主要目的是帮助学生为分布式和异构环境中的大数据建模和大规模数据管理做好准备。一方面，学习大数据管理的原理将为学生成为数据科学家的职业做好准备，不受不断的技术变革的影响。特别是，学习如何建模、查询和集成大数据是为分析目的准备数据的必要技能。例如，PageRank 等 MapReduce 算法说明了如何有效地对数十亿网页进行排名。另一方面，研究当前的大数据技术将展示当今什么是可能的、什么是不可能的，同时也突出了未来工作的机会。例如，Spark提供了一个用于准备和分析大数据的集成技术框架，而颠覆性的区块链技术则体现了一种具有高容错性的分布式计算系统，其应用潜力我们才刚刚开始了解。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 753","title":"海量数据算法","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20753","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 753 - Algorithms for Massive Data","prescription":"Modern enterprises and applications such as electronic commerce, social networks, location services, and scientific databases are generating data on a massive scale. Analysis of such data must be carried out by scalable algorithms. This course exposes data science practitioners and researchers to various advanced algorithms for processing and mining massive data, and explores best-practices and state-of-the-art developments in big data. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 320","overview":"Modern applications such as electronic commerce, social networks, and location services are expecting efficient big data solutions. This course exposes practitioners to computational bottlenecks of processing, managing, and mining big data. It introduces a wide spectrum of advanced algorithmic techniques that underpin big data analytics and knowledge discovery. Learning advanced algorithms of big data will prepare students for a career as data scientists, and big data engineers. In particular, techniques to model and answer queries in streaming data are necessary skills for applications such as the Internet of Things. Algorithms to scale up machine learning models used in recommender systems or social network analytics are key algorithmic ingredients in big data analytics. This is one of the core courses in the MProf Studs Data Science and Master of Data Science programs. Students of the Honours program and the Master of Information Technology can also participate in this course. There are no formal prerequisites required, but we recommend that students should already take COMPSCI 320 or relevant courses since it helps with the understanding and contextualization of the principles, techniques, and algorithm complexity covered in this course.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect: 3 hours of lectures per week Two-hour tutorial for each topic An average of 4 hours per week for revising the course content and solving assignments and/or test preparation","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MProfStuds - Master of Professional Studies","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"电子商务、社交网络和位置服务等现代应用都需要高效的大数据解决方案。本课程向从业者展示处理、管理和挖掘大数据的计算瓶颈。它引入了支持大数据分析和知识发现的各种先进算法技术。学习大数据的高级算法将为学生成为数据科学家和大数据工程师的职业做好准备。特别是，对流数据中的查询进行建模和回答的技术是物联网等应用程序的必要技能。用于扩展推荐系统或社交网络分析中使用的机器学习模型的算法是大数据分析中的关键算法成分。这是数据科学硕士和数据科学硕士课程的核心课程之一。荣誉课程和信息技术硕士的学生也可以参加本课程。没有正式的先决条件要求，但我们建议学生应该已经学习 COMPSCI 320 或相关课程，因为它有助于理解本课程所涵盖的原理、技术和算法复杂性并结合上下文。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 760","title":"机器学习高级主题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20760","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 760 - Advanced Topics in Machine Learning","prescription":"An overview of the learning problem and the view of learning by search. Covers advanced techniques for learning such as: decision tree learning, rule learning, exhaustive learning, Bayesian learning, genetic algorithms, reinforcement learning, neural networks, explanation-based learning and inductive logic programming. Advanced experimental methods necessary for understanding machine learning research.","overview":"Machine learning techniques are widely used in many computing applications; for example, in web search engines, spam filtering, speech and image recognition, computer games, machine vision, credit card fraud detection, stock market analysis and product marketing applications. Machine learning implies that there is some improvement that results from the learning program having seen some data. The improvement can be in terms of some performance program (e.g., learning an expert system or improving the performance of a planning or scheduling program), in terms of finding an unknown relation in the data (e.g., data mining, pattern analysis), or in terms of customizing adaptive systems (e.g., adaptive user-interfaces or adaptive agents). This course will introduce recent developments in the field of machine learning, and it is research-oriented. The practical component of this course involves working on a real-world research project developed with the help of the teaching team. The research project involves the definition of research questions, project planning, data analysis workflow, programming, collaboration and regular communication of project progress in an oral or written form, including writing a literature review and a final research report. Programming skills are necessary for this course. The practical component of the course expects group work.","topics":"The course will cover advanced topics in Machine Learning such as (but not limited to): Advanced neural networks Algorithmic fairness Adversarial learning Probabilistic graphical models","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend about 150 hours in total. Expected weekly workload: 1-hour lecture 1-hour lecture review 1-2 hour reading and thinking 6-7 hours for the research project/ assignments Students are expected to spend additional 30 hours on exam preparation","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"机器学习技术广泛应用于许多计算应用中；例如，在网络搜索引擎、垃圾邮件过滤、语音和图像识别、计算机游戏、机器视觉、信用卡欺诈检测、股票市场分析和产品营销应用中。机器学习意味着学习程序看到一些数据后会产生一些改进。改进可以是在某些性能程序方面（例如，学习专家系统或提高计划或调度程序的性能），在发现数据中的未知关系方面（例如，数据挖掘、模式分析），或在定制自适应系统方面（例如，自适应用户界面或自适应代理）。本课程将介绍机器学习领域的最新进展，并且是研究型的。本课程的实践部分涉及在教学团队的帮助下开发的现实世界研究项目。研究项目涉及研究问题的定义、项目规划、数据分析工作流程、编程、协作以及以口头或书面形式定期交流项目进展，包括撰写文献综述和最终研究报告。本课程需要编程技能。该课程的实践部分需要小组合作。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 761","title":"人工智能高级主题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20761","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 761 - Advanced Topics in Artificial Intelligence","prescription":"Examines the cornerstones of AI: representation, utilisation and acquisition of knowledge. Taking a real-world problem and representing it in a computer so that the computer can do inference. Utilising this knowledge and acquiring new knowledge is done by search which is the main technique behind planning and machine learning. Research frontiers in artificial intelligence.","overview":"This course will cover the representation and utilisation of knowledge. These are the cornerstones of AI. You will investigate how to take a real-world problem and represent it in a computer so that the computer can solve that problem. Utilising this knowledge is done by search. The basics of search and its use in planning and in-game playing will be covered. The course will consist of four modules: Module 1: Search. Many AI problems can be solved in principle by carefully crafted search algorithms. One can even argue that most AI tasks, such as problem-solving, learning, and planning, involve some type of search. This module covers fundamental search algorithms that include uninformed and informed search strategies as well as heuristics, which are \"rules of thumb\" for prioritising search options. We will also cover basic adversarial search strategies useful for multi-agent/game contexts. Module 2: Logic. Logic is an important tool to capture the formal reasoning and problem-solving of an artificial agent. Many successful applications of AI are based on logic. The course will examine different logical languages and use Prolog as a tool to apply logic deduction to AI problems. Logic will also be important in knowledge representation as a general language to describe and reason about knowledge, both with and without uncertainty. Module 3: Planning. Planning is a crucial ability of an intelligent agent when it is able to set goals and execute them. This includes developing a representation of the state of the world and making predictions about how decisions will affect the world states. In this course, we will examine important planning algorithms in artificial intelligence. Module 4: Reasoning with Uncertainty: Uncertainty is a key feature of real-world problems. An AI system needs to be able to incorporate uncertainty into its reasoning mechanism. To achieve this, we will describe models such as Bayesian networks and Markov chains that facilitate probabilistic reasoning. We will also introduce utility theory and decision networks that allow an AI system to make optimal decisions. Module 5: Natural language processing. NLP refers to the ability to read and comprehend human language. This is a crucial ability of an intelligent agent in communicating with humans. Applications of NLP include information retrieval, text mining, question answering and machine translation. This course will go over these applications as well as fundamental tools. The knowledge taught in this course is essential for any student who has an interest in artificial intelligence. The skills developed in this course are particularly useful for those wishing to have a career involving artificial intelligence.","topics":"Logic Prolog Search Adversarial search Constraint satisfaction Knowledge representation Inference Planning Reasoning with uncertainty Natural language processing","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect: 3 hours of lectures 4 hours of reading and thinking about the content 3-4 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程将涵盖知识的表示和利用。这些是人工智能的基石。您将研究如何处理现实世界的问题并将其表示在计算机中，以便计算机可以解决该问题。利用这些知识是通过搜索来完成的。将涵盖搜索的基础知识及其在规划和游戏中的使用。该课程将包含四个模块： 模块 1：搜索。许多人工智能问题原则上可以通过精心设计的搜索算法来解决。人们甚至可以说，大多数人工智能任务，例如解决问题、学习和规划，都涉及某种类型的搜索。该模块涵盖了基本的搜索算法，其中包括无信息和有信息的搜索策略以及启发式算法，这些是用于确定搜索选项优先级的“经验法则”。我们还将介绍对多代理/游戏环境有用的基本对抗性搜索策略。模块 2：逻辑。逻辑是捕捉人工智能体的形式推理和问题解决的重要工具。人工智能的许多成功应用都是基于逻辑的。该课程将考察不同的逻辑语言，并使用 Prolog 作为工具将逻辑演绎应用于人工智能问题。逻辑在知识表示中也很重要，作为描述和推理知识的通用语言，无论有没有不确定性。模块 3：规划。当智能代理能够设定目标并执行目标时，规划是其一项至关重要的能力。这包括开发世界状态的表示并预测决策将如何影响世界状态。在本课程中，我们将研究人工智能中重要的规划算法。模块 4：不确定性推理：不确定性是现实世界问题的一个关键特征。人工智能系统需要能够将不确定性纳入其推理机制。为了实现这一目标，我们将描述促进概率推理的模型，例如贝叶斯网络和马尔可夫链。我们还将介绍效用理论和决策网络，使人工智能系统能够做出最佳决策。模块 5：自然语言处理。 NLP 是指阅读和理解人类语言的能力。这是智能体与人类交流的关键能力。 NLP 的应用包括信息检索、文本挖掘、问答和机器翻译。本课程将介绍这些应用程序以及基本工具。本课程教授的知识对于任何对人工智能感兴趣的学生来说都是必不可少的。本课程中培养的技能对于那些希望从事人工智能相关职业的人特别有用。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 762","title":"机器学习基础","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20762","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 762 - Foundations of Machine Learning","prescription":"Machine learning is a branch of artificial intelligence concerned with making accurate, interpretable, computationally efficient, and robust inferences from data to solve a given problem. Students will be introduced to the foundations of machine learning and will gain practical skills to solve different problems. Students will explore research frontiers in machine learning.","overview":"This course will provide the foundations of machine learning. Students will explore what it means to learn models from data (without explicitly coding knowledge), including concepts from computational and statistical learning theory to understand, reason and potentially develop new methods for automated learning. Through this course, students will learn the fundamental algorithmic principles, and the challenges, involved in getting computers to learn from data, as well as develop practical skills to solve different learning problems and the ability to critically evaluate the modelling results. This will allow students to specialise further in advanced areas of data science, machine learning or artificial intelligence. This course provides a broad introduction to machine learning. The course expects mastery of fundamental mathematical skills, foundations in statistical analysis and concepts, good programming skills (the preferred programming language for this course is Python), and a good understanding of algorithm design and computational complexity. NOTE: For this course you need programming skills in Python, basic linear algebra and basic statistical knowledge.","topics":"Fundamentals of machine learning theory (e.g. bias/variance theory), model evaluation and validation (e.g., cross-validation, model performance measures such as accuracy, precision, recall, ROC/AUC) Supervised learning methods (e.g., decision trees, support vector machines, neural networks) Unsupervised learning methods (e.g., clustering)","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect: 3 hours of lectures A 1-hour tutorial 3 hours of reading and thinking about the content 3 hours of work on assignments per week As well as, approximately 30 hours for test/exam preparation across the semester.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"MProfStuds - Master of Professional Studies","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程将提供机器学习的基础。学生将探索从数据中学习模型（无需明确编码知识）意味着什么，包括计算和统计学习理论中的概念，以理解、推理和潜在地开发自动学习的新方法。通过本课程，学生将学习基本算法原理以及让计算机从数据中学习所面临的挑战，并培养解决不同学习问题的实践技能以及批判性评估建模结果的能力。这将使学生能够进一步专注于数据科学、机器学习或人工智能的先进领域。本课程对机器学习进行了广泛的介绍。该课程要求学生掌握基本的数学技能、统计分析和概念的基础、良好的编程技能（本课程的首选编程语言是 Python）以及对算法设计和计算复杂性的良好理解。注意：对于本课程，您需要 Python 编程技能、基本线性代数和基本统计​​知识。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 765","title":"建模思维","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20765","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 765 - Modelling Minds","prescription":"How can researchers of artificial intelligence effectively model subjective aspects of minds, such as emotional states, desires, perceptual experience and intrinsic goals? This course draws upon interdisciplinary methods and considers classic and emerging approaches to try to answer this question. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 367","overview":"Artificial intelligence is progressing at a tremendous rate. In many contexts, it outperforms humans. Yet key differences remain between AI and natural minds. What are these differences? What can biology and cognitive science teach us about AI, and conversely, what can AI teach us about natural forms of intelligence? This course aims to highlight the interdisciplinary nature of AI research. In it, we will build computational models to contrast different approaches to building AI. We will focus on perspectives in modern AI and cognitive science that emphasize the dynamical, embodied and situated nature of natural intelligence, as these are properties that may help us make more life-like AI and improve our understanding of what we are as thinking, living beings.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 3 hours of class time per week including a mixture of lectures, reading-based seminars and exercises. The remaining 7 hours per week will be spent reading academic articles, and working on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MProfStuds - Master of Professional Studies","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"人工智能正在以惊人的速度进步。在许多情况下，它的表现优于人类。然而，人工智能和自然思维之间仍然存在关键差异。这些差异是什么？生物学和认知科学可以教给我们什么关于人工智能的知识，反过来，人工智能可以教我们什么关于自然形式的智能？本课程旨在强调人工智能研究的跨学科性质。在其中，我们将构建计算模型来对比构建人工智能的不同方法。我们将重点关注现代人工智能和认知科学的观点，这些观点强调自然智能的动态性、体现性和情境性，因为这些特性可以帮助我们创造出更加逼真的人工智能，并提高我们对我们作为有思想的生物的理解。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 767","title":"智能软件代理","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20767","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 767 - Intelligent Software Agents","prescription":"An introduction to the design, implementation and use of intelligent software agents (e.g., knowbots, softbots, etc). Reviews standard artificial intelligence problem-solving paradigms (e.g., planning and expert systems) and knowledge representation formalisms (e.g., logic and semantic nets). Surveys agent architectures and multi-agent frameworks.","overview":"This course will look at intelligent software agents both in multi-agent settings and with respect to an individual intelligent software agent. A multi-agent system (MAS) consists of multiple interacting agents who share the same environment. Such systems are useful for multi-party, complex, real-time problems with uncertainty that are often impossible or hard to model or solve using a single agent alone. Applications of multi-agent systems range from negotiation, cooperating robots, market and auction analysis, to security. A main theme in this field involves strategic agents where game theory is an important tool. We will be looking at the algorithmic and game-theoretic foundations of multi-agent systems in this course. We will also be considering multi-agent systems composed of humans, AI agents and human organisations, and methods for developing effective joint behaviours. One of the core abilities of an intelligent agent is to be able to solve problems. Search is a general purpose technique for finding solutions to problems. However, these search spaces can be quite large and we need to be able to reduce the size of the search space in order to solve problems in a reasonable amount of time and space. We will be explore reducing the size of these search spaces. The goal of this course is to understand techniques, applications and implications of intelligent agents and multi-agent and mixed multi-agent systems ; a major technique toward this goal is to improve your ability to quickly read and evaluate complex technical papers, integrating and communicating the results.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week. For this course, you can expect 36 hours of lectures and 72 hours of reading and thinking and discussing the content and 12 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation. These workload expectations are estimates only, and may vary.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程将探讨多代理设置中的智能软件代理以及单个智能软件代理。多代理系统 (MAS) 由共享同一环境的多个交互代理组成。此类系统对于具有不确定性的多方、复杂、实时问题非常有用，这些问题通常不可能或难以单独使用单个代理来建模或解决。多代理系统的应用范围从谈判、协作机器人、市场和拍卖分析到安全。该领域的一个主题涉及战略代理，其中博弈论是一个重要的工具。在本课程中，我们将研究多智能体系统的算法和博弈论基础。我们还将考虑由人类、人工智能代理和人类组织组成的多代理系统，以及开发有效联合行为的方法。智能代理的核心能力之一是能够解决问题。搜索是一种寻找问题解决方案的通用技术。然而，这些搜索空间可能非常大，我们需要能够减小搜索空间的大小，以便在合理的时间和空间内解决问题。我们将探索减少这些搜索空间的大小。本课程的目标是了解智能代理、多代理和混合多代理系统的技术、应用和含义；实现这一目标的一项主要技术是提高快速阅读和评估复杂技术论文、整合和交流结果的能力。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 771","title":"计算机图形学与图像处理高级主题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20771","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 771 - Advanced Topics in Computer Graphics and Image Processing","prescription":"Basic geometric processes including transformations; viewing and projection; back projection and ray tracing. Graphics modelling concepts: primitives, surfaces, and scene graphs, lighting and shading, texture mapping, and curve and surface design. Graphics and image processing fundamentals: image definition and representation, perception and colour models, grey level and colour enhancement, neighbourhood operations and filtering. Use of the OpenGL graphics pipeline. Research frontiers in computer graphics and image processing. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 210, 230","overview":"","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":""},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSCI 773","title":"智能视觉系统","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20773","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 773 - Intelligent Vision Systems","prescription":"Computational methods and techniques for computer vision are applied to real-world problems such as 2/3D face biometrics, autonomous navigation, and vision-guided robotics based on 3D scene description. A particular feature of the course work is the emphasis on complete system design. Recommended preparation: COMPSCI 373 and 15 points at Stage II in Mathematics","overview":"This course introduces computational methods and techniques for computer vision, extended towards real-world problems such as vision-guided robotics based on 3D scene description and features for learning (including deep-learning examples). Quizzes and/or programming assignments will support the course materials via online delivery. In-class or online tests (two hours each) will be held in weeks 6 and 12. Building upon COMPSCI 373 contents (or see recommended reading \"G. Gimel'farb, P. Delmas; Image Processing and Analysis: A Primer\"), COMPSCI 773 delivers a more in-depth understanding of established and state-of-the-art computer vision methodology and research, which is valuable for all students including those studying towards honours, MSc and PhD degrees. Furthermore, computer vision techniques are an area where specialists are sought by industry (e.g. artificial intelligence, robotics, gaming or augmented reality), both nationally as well as internationally.","topics":"Computer vision Camera calibration and image registration Image features for learning and matching Machine learning-based methods","workload":"This is a standard 15-point course, and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect: 2 hours of lectures A 1-hour lecture/tutorial 2 hours of reading and thinking about the content 5 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程介绍计算机视觉的计算方法和技术，并扩展到现实世界的问题，例如基于 3D 场景描述和学习特征（包括深度学习示例）的视觉引导机器人技术。测验和/或编程作业将通过在线交付支持课程材料。课堂或在线测试（每次两小时）将在第 6 周和第 12 周举行。以 COMPSCI 373 内容为基础（或参阅推荐读物“G. Gimel'farb、P. Delmas；图像处理和分析：入门”），COMPSCI 773 提供对既定且最先进的计算机视觉方法和研究的更深入理解，这对所有学生（包括攻读荣誉学位、理学硕士和博士学位的学生）都很有价值。此外，计算机视觉技术是国内外行业（例如人工智能、机器人、游戏或增强现实）寻求专家的领域。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 701","title":"高级数字系统设计","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20701","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 701 - Advanced Digital Systems Design - Level 9","prescription":"Advanced concepts in digital design including: System-on-Chip (system level description, behavioural and register-transfer descriptions); advanced modelling techniques and design flows; design space exploration and optimisation; hardware-software partitioning and trade-offs; component reusability; reconfigurable systems; low-power systems; case studies (speech, image, video algorithms implementation, application specific processor design); individual research projects to analyse the problem, model and implement the required hardware-software components.","overview":"This is an advanced, research-oriented and project-based course with extensive hands-on experience. Increasing design complexity and advances in semiconductor technology provide the opportunity to have all parts of a digital system as a System on a Chip (SOC), where parts of system functionality are developed as software and parts as hardware following Hardware/Software partitioning paradigm. Also, it is necessary to be able to design, re-use and exchange Intellectual Property (IP) (both hardware and software) in a systematic, reliable and easy way. Therefore, advanced methodologies and tools are needed on a system-level for digital system design, validation and verification at different levels of abstraction. System-level design can help cope with the growing complexity of both the hardware and software. While software design is based on the use of high-level programming languages with design cycle that allows relatively easy and quick modifications and revisions, hardware design deals with much lower level of abstraction and requires specific design expertise, including the knowledge of the architecture on which hardware will be mapped and implemented. This results in achieving shorter time to market for systems in addition to providing better opportunities for quality system design and verification. As most of the designs require final synthesizable design representation, hardware description languages, such as VHDL and Verilog, are most often used for the final design. A refresher of VHDL and register-transfer level design will be made in this course and then the language will be used to target relatively complex designs, such as complete processor or heterogeneous multi-core SoC, on one hand, or behaviourally specified algorithms, on the other hand. Both these directions of digital systems design will be further analysed and their use in real designs demonstrated. This includes using FPGAs as the target implementation technology that allows the development of specialised reconfigurable chips. Also, aspects of the synthesis from the high level specifications (High-Level Synthesis, HLS) using programming language inputs and their synthesis into hardware accelerators or application-specific processors will be discussed. The course is completely assessed by the coursework. Assessment of the work on project is through a number of checkpoints and final report and presentation in the class.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week on average involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. This includes study break weeks. For each week in this course, you can expect 2 hours of lectures, one hour of tutorial and/or labs, and the remaining hours are supposed to be used for reading, thinking, designing parts of the systems, writing reports and demonstrating your solutions to the class.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"MEngSt - Master of Engineering Studies","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"这是一门先进的、以研究为导向、基于项目的课程，具有丰富的实践经验。设计复杂性的增加和半导体技术的进步提供了将数字系统的所有部分都作为片上系统（SOC）的机会，其中系统功能的一部分作为软件开发，而部分作为硬件开发，遵循硬件/软件分区范例。此外，还必须能够以系统、可靠和简单的方式设计、重复使用和交换知识产权（IP）（包括硬件和软件）。因此，需要在系统级上使用先进的方法和工具来进行不同抽象级别的数字系统设计、验证和验证。系统级设计可以帮助应对硬件和软件日益增长的复杂性。虽然软件设计基于高级编程语言的使用，其设计周期允许相对容易和快速的修改和修订，但硬件设计处理的抽象级别要低得多，并且需要特定的设计专业知识，包括硬件映射和实现的架构知识。这不仅可以缩短系统的上市时间，还可以为高质量系统设计和验证提供更好的机会。由于大多数设计需要最终可综合的设计表示，因此最终设计最常使用硬件描述语言，例如 VHDL 和 Verilog。本课程将复习 VHDL 和寄存器传输级设计，然后该语言将用于针对相对复杂的设计，例如一方面是完整的处理器或异构多核 SoC，另一方面是行为指定的算法。数字系统设计的这两个方向都将得到进一步分析，并展示它们在实际设计中的应用。这包括使用 FPGA 作为目标实现技术，允许开发专用的可重构芯片。此外，还将讨论使用编程语言输入进行高级规范（高级综合，HLS）综合以及将其综合到硬件加速器或专用处理器中的各个方面。该课程完全由课程作业来评估。对项目工作的评估是通过一些检查点以及最终报告和课堂演示进行的。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 704","title":"高级嵌入式系统","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20704","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 704 - Advanced Embedded Systems - Level 9","prescription":"Selected advanced topics from current research in embedded systems such as: embedded systems based on formal models of computation; centralised and distributed architectures for embedded systems; static and dynamic embedded systems; languages and frameworks for distributed embedded systems; actor and agent systems; verification. Includes a significant individual research project.","overview":"The overarching theme of the course is technologies related to Internet of Things (IoT) and Industrial IoT (IIoT) and approaches to use them in creation of future systems in application domains such as industrial automation, building automation, robotics, health informatics, precision agriculture, to name a few. This course aims to cover and provide students insights to selected topics in advanced embedded systems that are underlying technology of IoT and IIoT. It includes concepts such as formal models of computation for embedded (centralised and distributed) systems, programming and design languages and approaches based on those concepts, advanced architectures of multiprocessors and networks-on-chip, and embedded wireless sensor and actuator networks and technologies for streaming applications based on various (small to very large, \"big data\") scale sensing and actuation. Students will undertake a small scale individual research project on selected topic of their choice and get familiar with the state of the art in research and practice. In addition to the fundamental concepts, students will also gain hands on experience on embedded systems modelling and design using state of the art technologies in two design projects drawn from real-life applications.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week, including study weeks, involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For each week in this course, you can expect 2 hours of lectures and an hour tutorial or lab per week, and the remaining hours are supposed to be used for reading, thinking, designing parts of the systems and writing reports.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"MEngSt - Master of Engineering Studies","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程的首要主题是与物联网 (IoT) 和工业物联网 (IIoT) 相关的技术以及使用这些技术在工业自动化、楼宇自动化、机器人、健康信息学、精准农业等应用领域创建未来系统的方法。本课程旨在涵盖并为学生提供对作为物联网和工业物联网基础技术的高级嵌入式系统中选定主题的见解。它包括诸如嵌入式（集中式和分布式）系统的形式计算模型、基于这些概念的编程和设计语言和方法、多处理器和片上网络的高级架构、嵌入式无线传感器和执行器网络以及基于各种（从小到非常大的“大数据”）规模传感和驱动的流应用程序的技术等概念。学生将就自己选择的选定主题进行小规模的个人研究项目，并熟悉研究和实践的最新技术。除了基本概念之外，学生还将在两个来自现实应用的设计项目中使用最先进的技术获得嵌入式系统建模和设计的实践经验。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 705","title":"安全关键软件的形式方法","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20705","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 705 - Formal Methods for Safety Critical Software - Level 9","prescription":"Formal methods for the validation/verification of safety critical software, including machine learning algorithms. Topics covered will include mathematical modelling for embedded, automation, and mechatronic systems; advanced techniques for validation and verification; techniques for formal specification; methods of verification such as Bisimulation and model checking; state space explosion problem and solutions such as BDDs, symbolic model checking, and modular verification; verification of HDL/C using model checking tools. Includes a significant individual research project.","overview":"This is an interdisciplinary course inspired by software engineering and formal methods. However, this course has been adapted for applicability for computer systems engineering, mechatronics, bio- engineering and medical devices domains. Executable biology refers to executable computational models that mimic biological processes. In this course we will present a range of executable models inspired by formal methods especially for medical device validation. The range of formal methods considered here include a synchronous Statechart called SCChart for pacemaker modelling, a long with a number of tools for formal verification such as: (1) Uppaal for pacemaker verification, (2) easy-rte for runtime verification and validation, (3) SPIN, which will be used for model checking concurrent programs, and finally (4) Satisfiability Modulo Theory based tool called Z3 from Microsoft, which will be used for program equivalence checking. The course is taught in two parts: Part 1-Fundamentals of model-based design founded on formal methods. • Automatic verification of closed and open systems using model checking and module checking. • Modelling and verification of a pacemaker using UPPAAL. • Run-time verification and enforcement of safety-critical systems. • Formal modelling and verification using process algebras and bisimulation. • Introduction to Cyber-Physical Systems (CPS). Part 2-Industrial successes of formal methods • Linear Temporal Logic and its industrial applications • Satisfiability Modulo Theory • Proof carrying code • Correctness of software programs","topics":"Model checking, Satisfability modulo Theory, Concurrency, Formal verification, Runtime validation.","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For each week in this course, you can expect 3 hours of lectures, up to 1 hour tutorial and labs, while the remaining time is spent on reading, thinking, developing solutions for the assignments and preparations for the test.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"MEngSt - Master of Engineering Studies","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"这是一门受软件工程和形式化方法启发的跨学科课程。然而，本课程已针对计算机系统工程、机电一体化、生物工程和医疗设备领域的适用性进行了调整。可执行生物学是指模仿生物过程的可执行计算模型。在本课程中，我们将展示一系列受正式方法启发的可执行模型，特别是针对医疗设备验证。这里考虑的形式化方法的范围包括用于起搏器建模的称为 SCChart 的同步 Statechart，以及用于形式化验证的许多工具，例如：（1）用于起搏器验证的 Uppaal，（2）用于运行时验证和验证的 easy-rte，（3）SPIN，它将用于模型检查并发程序，最后（4）来自 Microsoft 的名为 Z3 的基于可满足性模理论的工具，它将用于程序等效性检查。本课程分为两部分教授： 第 1 部分 - 基于形式方法的基于模型的设计基础知识。 • 使用模型检查和模块检查自动验证封闭式和开放式系统。 • 使用UPPAAL 对起搏器进行建模和验证。 • 安全关键系统的运行时验证和执行。 • 使用过程代数和互模拟进行形式化建模和验证。 • 网络物理系统(CPS) 简介。第 2 部分 - 形式化方法的工业成功 • 线性时态逻辑及其工业应用 • 可满足性模理论 • 证明携带代码 • 软件程序的正确性"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 708","title":"专题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20708","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 708 - Special Topic","prescription":"An advanced course on topics to be determined each year by the Head of Department.","overview":"This course is designed for students keen to explore emerging or niche areas. Learning outcomes include advanced knowledge in a specialised subject area, critically evaluating current research and practice, and communicating ideas in written reports and/or oral presentations.","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BE(Hons) - Bachelor of Engineering (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程专为热衷于探索新兴或利基领域的学生而设计。学习成果包括专业学科领域的先进知识、批判性地评估当前的研究和实践，以及在书面报告和/或口头演示中交流想法。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 710","title":"计算机系统工程研究 1","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20710","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 710 - Studies in Computer Systems Engineering 1","prescription":"Advanced courses on topics to be determined each year by the Head of Department.","overview":"","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2024","programme":"MEngSt - Master of Engineering Studies","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":""},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 711","title":"计算机系统工程研究 2","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20711","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 711 - Studies in Computer Systems Engineering 2","prescription":"Advanced courses on topics to be determined each year by the Head of Department.","overview":"","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MEngSt - Master of Engineering Studies","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":""},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 713","title":"计算机系统工程研究 4","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20713","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 713 - Studies in Computer Systems Engineering 4","prescription":"Advanced courses on topics to be determined each year by the Head of Department.","overview":"","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MEngSt - Master of Engineering Studies","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":""},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 714","title":"计算机系统工程研究 5","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20714","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 714 - Studies in Computer Systems Engineering 5","prescription":"Advanced courses on topics to be determined each year by the Head of Department.","overview":"","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MEngSt - Master of Engineering Studies","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":""},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 715","title":"计算机系统工程研究 6","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20715","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 715 - Studies in Computer Systems Engineering 6","prescription":"Advanced courses on topics to be determined each year by the Head of Department.","overview":"","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MEngSt - Master of Engineering Studies","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":""},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 721","title":"机器学习与深度学习","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20721","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 721 - Machine Intelligence and Deep Learning","prescription":"Explores essential concepts and technologies in state-of-the-art deep neural network architectures, including convolutional neural networks, decision trees, random forests, similarity learning, recurrent neural networks, and long short-term memory networks. Includes hands-on experience combining hardware components with software implementations.","overview":"The class \"Machine Intelligence and Deep Learning\" (COMPSYS721) overviews essential concepts and technologies in deep neural network architectures. During the 12-week course, students will learn to implement and train their neural networks and gain a detailed understanding of cutting-edge research in machine learning and deep neural networks. Furthermore, through the labs and the course projects, students will develop the toolset for setting up deep learning tasks and practical engineering tricks for training and fine-tuning deep neural networks. The final project will be on training the Jetson nano module to do object tracking and face recognition tasks. The course will mainly discuss the following topics: Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs): Students delve into CNN principles, advanced methods, and hands-on practice to equip them for applying CNNs in real-world situations. The curriculum covers both theoretical and practical implementation using Python and popular frameworks. Decision Trees and Random Forests: Students learn how these algorithms organize data for predictions, with Random Forests expanding on Decision Trees to handle more intricate data sets while enhancing accuracy. Similarity Learning with Siamese Neural Networks: This section introduces how to gauge object similarity using Siamese Networks utilizing machine learning techniques. It explores applications like face recognition, examines loss functions like Triplet loss, and discusses One Shot and Few Shot Learning. Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) and LSTM: The course explores RNNs tailored for time series data, introducing LSTM networks to tackle long-term dependencies for improved performance in tasks like language translation and time series forecasting. Throughout the course, participants engage in lab exercises and real-world scenarios to practice their skills in applying these algorithms to machine learning challenges. There will be projects using hardware components and software implementations.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 3 hours of lectures, a 1 hour tutorial, 3 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 5 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"BE(Hons) - Bachelor of Engineering (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"“机器智能和深度学习”(COMPSYS721) 课程概述了深度神经网络架构中的基本概念和技术。在为期 12 周的课程中，学生将学习如何实现和训练神经网络，并详细了解机器学习和深度神经网络的前沿研究。此外，通过实验室和课程项目，学生将开发用于设置深度学习任务的工具集以及用于训练和微调深度神经网络的实用工程技巧。最终项目将是训练 Jetson nano 模块执行对象跟踪和人脸识别任务。课程将主要讨论以下主题： 卷积神经网络（CNN）：学生深入研究 CNN 原理、高级方法和实践，以帮助他们在实际情况中应用 CNN。课程涵盖使用 Python 和流行框架的理论和实践实施。决策树和随机森林：学生学习这些算法如何组织数据进行预测，随机森林在决策树的基础上进行扩展，以处理更复杂的数据集，同时提高准确性。使用连体神经网络进行相似性学习：本节介绍如何利用机器学习技术使用连体网络来衡量对象相似性。它探讨了人脸识别等应用，检查了 Triplet loss 等损失函数，并讨论了 One Shot 和 Few Shot Learning。循环神经网络 (RNN) 和 LSTM：本课程探讨了为时间序列数据量身定制的 RNN，引入 LSTM 网络来解决长期依赖性，从而提高语言翻译和时间序列预测等任务的性能。在整个课程中，参与者参与实验室练习和现实场景，以练习将这些算法应用于机器学习挑战的技能。将有使用硬件组件和软件实现的项目。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 722","title":"专题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20722","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 722 - Special Topic","prescription":"An advanced course on topics to be determined each year by the Head of Department.","overview":"This course is designed for students keen to explore emerging or niche areas. Learning outcomes include advanced knowledge in a specialised subject area, critically evaluating current research and practice, and communicating ideas in written reports and/or oral presentations.","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BE(Hons) - Bachelor of Engineering (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程专为热衷于探索新兴或利基领域的学生而设计。学习成果包括专业学科领域的先进知识、批判性地评估当前的研究和实践，以及在书面报告和/或口头演示中交流想法。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 723","title":"嵌入式系统设计","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20723","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 723 - Embedded Systems Design","prescription":"Concurrency and models of computation, task models and race conditions, real-time operating systems based approach, synchronous approach, safe state machines, key properties: determinism and reactivity, SoPC and MPSoC, cyber-physical embedded systems, static analysis techniques, case studies in smart grid, automotive, medical devices and the like.","overview":"In this course students will get exposure to the management of concurrency and real-time nature of embedded systems using two distinct high-level approaches: the real-time operating systems approach and the system-level design approach. These approaches are essential for the design of complex embedded systems. Several real-life examples (power system frequency measurement and relay, cruise controllers, and lift controllers) will be used in this course to contrast the two approaches. The course is divided into two parts as follows. Part I Embedded Systems Design In this part of the course the focus is on the concepts of concurrency as the way to tackle complexity of the embedded systems. We will discuss the role of hardware and software approaches to implement concurrency and then focus on systems that are designed using software approaches only. The topics that will be covered in this part include (1) embedded systems design approaches and design flows, typical steps in the design process, the ways of organising hardware and software for embedded and real-time systems and approaches to deal with concurrency (scheduling, resource protection, task synchronisation and communication), (2) concurrency and formal models of computation (MoC) as the basis for the design of reliable and trustworthy embedded systems (different variants of state machines, data flow graphs and their combinations, Globally Asynchronous Locally Synchronous (GALS) MoC), and (3) software-implemented embedded systems based on the use the C sequential programming language and its extension that supports concurrency in the form of a Real-Time Operating System (RTOS), as well as those implemented using concurrent programming languages. In this part, students will get familiarised with a commercial grade RTOS and use it in a group project (assignment 1) designing a real-life hard real-time system that uses the Intel FPGA-based soft-core processor and FPGA hardware resources. Part II Executable specifications We will learn about the synchronous approach to embedded system design. In particular, the Esterel and Safe State Machines (SSMs) design paradigms will be introduced and used. We will discuss in detail issues related to safety, real-time, compilation and formal analysis. We will examine issues related to real-time networking using the time-triggered protocol (TTP). Many illustrative examples will be used throughout this part. In this part of the course students will also do an assignment involving the synchronous approach (assignment 2).","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. It refers to the teaching and study break weeks of the semester as well as preparations for the exam. For each week in this course, you can expect 3 hours of lectures, up to 1 hour in average tutorials and labs, while the remaining time is spent on reading, thinking, and developing solutions for the assignments and preparations for the exam.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"BE(Hons) - Bachelor of Engineering (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"在本课程中，学生将使用两种不同的高级方法来了解嵌入式系统的并发管理和实时性：实时操作系统方法和系统级设计方法。这些方法对于复杂嵌入式系统的设计至关重要。本课程将使用几个现实生活中的示例（电力系统频率测量和继电器、巡航控制器和电梯控制器）来对比这两种方法。本课程分为以下两个部分。第一部分 嵌入式系统设计 在课程的这一部分中，重点是并发概念，作为解决嵌入式系统复杂性的方法。我们将讨论硬件和软件方法在实现并发方面的作用，然后重点讨论仅使用软件方法设计的系统。本部分将涵盖的主题包括（1）嵌入式系统设计方法和设计流程、设计过程中的典型步骤、组织嵌入式和实时系统的硬件和软件的方法以及处理并发性的方法（调度、资源保护、任务同步和通信），（2）并发性和计算的形式模型（MoC）作为可靠和值得信赖的嵌入式系统设计的基础（状态机的不同变体、数据流图及其组合、全局异步局部同步） （GALS）MoC），以及（3）基于使用 C 顺序编程语言及其扩展的软件实现的嵌入式系统，该扩展以实时操作系统（RTOS）的形式支持并发，以及使用并发编程语言实现的系统。在这一部分中，学生将熟悉商业级 RTOS 并在小组项目（作业 1）中使用它来设计一个使用基于英特尔 FPGA 的软核处理器和 FPGA 硬件资源的现实硬实时系统。第二部分 可执行规范 我们将了解嵌入式系统设计的同步方法。特别是，将介绍和使用 Esterel 和安全状态机 (SSM) 设计范例。我们将详细讨论与安全、实时、编译和形式分析相关的问题。我们将研究与使用时间触发协议（TTP）的实时网络相关的问题。本部分将使用许多说明性示例。在这部分课程中，学生还将完成一项涉及同步方法的作业（作业 2）。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 725","title":"分布式网络物理系统设计","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20725","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 725 - Distributed Cyber-Physical Systems Design","prescription":"Network layers and protocols. Packet switching. Broadband network principles. Low versus high bandwidth services. Network interfaces and instrumentation. Wireless networks in embedded applications. Industrial networking.","overview":"Knowledge of network protocols and distributed systems are becoming a pre-requisite to engineers, as all application we use/develop are part of large networks (and often distributed systems). In this course, an introduction to various network protocols will be addressed in detail. Further, a study of distributed networks will develop knowledge in the area of setting them up. Also, practical learning will happen in the form of labs and assignments that focus on real-life applications of computer networks.Group learning: In this course, group learning will be encouraged to facilitate ako (The Māori concept of ako means both to teach and to learn, and learning is viewed as a reciprocal process). The second assignment will be group-based to tackle a real-life computer networks problem, benefitting from group-learning.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 3 hours of lectures, a 2-hour tutorial and labs on average, while the remaining time is spent on reading, thinking, developing solutions for the assignments and preparations for the exam.","locations":"Summer Semester 2024","programme":"BE(Hons) - Bachelor of Engineering (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"了解网络协议和分布式系统正在成为工程师的先决条件，因为我们使用/开发的所有应用程序都是大型网络（通常是分布式系统）的一部分。在本课程中，将详细介绍各种网络协议。此外，对分布式网络的研究将发展建立分布式网络方面的知识。此外，实践学习将以实验室和作业的形式进行，重点关注计算机网络在现实生活中的应用。 小组学习：在本课程中，将鼓励小组学习以促进 ako（毛利人的 ako 概念意味着教学和学习，学习被视为一个相互的过程）。第二项任务将以小组为基础，通过小组学习来解决现实生活中的计算机网络问题。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 726","title":"机器人与智能系统","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20726","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 726 - Robotics and Intelligent Systems - Level 9","prescription":"Fundamentals of robotic and intelligent systems, including reactive and deliberative functionality, navigation techniques, planning and programming of robot actions, machine learning, artificial neural networks and may include topics in sensors and actuators, kinematic analysis, fuzzy systems, genetic algorithms. Core concepts are extended by an individual research project where a challenging robotics problem is analysed and a solution implemented and tested.","overview":"This course will focus on the principles and fundamentals of intelligent autonomous agents, of which a robot is a physical agent. The course covers the history of AI and Robotic development, from expert systems to modern deep learning-based perception and control. The course will explore how Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) can enhance and improve the autonomous behaviour of robots and other agents. Exploring intelligent perception, planning and control, and decision-making with a focus on Reinforcement Learning for learning autonomous behaviours. The course theory will be supplemented by hands-on practical assignments, where the students will learn to use AI and ML methods/tools to create their own autonomous agents through Expert Systems and Reinforcement Learning. By the end of the course, students should understand how to understand the fundamentals of AI/ML-based agents and be capable of applying AI and ML to real-world scenarios.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 36 hours of lectures, 10 hours of tutorials, 50 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 54 hours of work on assignments.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MEngSt - Master of Engineering Studies","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程将重点介绍智能自主代理的原理和基础知识，其中机器人是一个物理代理。该课程涵盖了人工智能和机器人发展的历史，从专家系统到基于现代深度学习的感知和控制。该课程将探讨人工智能 (AI) 和机器学习 (ML) 如何增强和改善机器人和其他智能体的自主行为。探索智能感知、规划和控制以及决策，重点是强化学习以学习自主行为。课程理论将辅以实践作业，学生将学习使用人工智能和机器学习方法/工具，通过专家系统和强化学习创建自己的自主代理。在课程结束时，学生应该了解如何理解基于人工智能/机器学习的代理的基础知识，并能够将人工智能和机器学习应用到现实场景中。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 727","title":"基于模型的嵌入式系统设计","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20727","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 727 - Model-based Embedded Systems Design - Level 9","prescription":"Traditional and advanced methods of embedded systems modelling and design, models of computation, hardware-software co-design, real-time and safety-critical systems, principles of embedded and real-time operating systems, design using the real-time operating systems approach and the synchronous approach, use of the networks in real-time embedded systems. The assessment includes a significant individual research project.","overview":"","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MEngSt - Master of Engineering Studies","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":""},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 728","title":"专题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20728","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 728 - Special Topic - Level 9","prescription":"An advanced course on a topic to be determined each year by the Head of Department. Includes a substantial individual research project.","overview":"This course is designed for students keen to explore emerging or niche areas. Learning outcomes include advanced knowledge in a specialised subject area, critically evaluating current research and practice, and communicating ideas in written reports and/or oral presentations.","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MEngSt - Master of Engineering Studies","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程专为热衷于探索新兴或利基领域的学生而设计。学习成果包括专业学科领域的先进知识、批判性地评估当前的研究和实践，以及在书面报告和/或口头演示中交流想法。"},{"group":"group1","code":"COMPSYS 729","title":"专题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSYS%20729","officialTitle":"COMPSYS 729 - Special Topic - Level 9","prescription":"An advanced course on a topic to be determined each year by the Head of Department. Includes a substantial individual research project.","overview":"This course is designed for students keen to explore emerging or niche areas. Learning outcomes include advanced knowledge in a specialised subject area, critically evaluating current research and practice, and communicating ideas in written reports and/or oral presentations.","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MEngSt - Master of Engineering Studies","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程专为热衷于探索新兴或利基领域的学生而设计。学习成果包括专业学科领域的先进知识、批判性地评估当前的研究和实践，以及在书面报告和/或口头演示中交流想法。"},{"group":"group1","code":"ELECTENG 722","title":"现代控制系统","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=ELECTENG%20722","officialTitle":"ELECTENG 722 - Modern Control Systems","prescription":"State space analysis, relationship to transfer function methods, controllability and observability, multivariable plant. Computer simulation. Stability considerations. State variable feedback. Digital control system, design and realisation of digital controllers, adaptive controllers. Nonlinear systems, phase-plane and describing function techniques, Lyapunov's method of stability analysis, design of controllers for non-linear systems. Variable structure systems.","overview":"Main contents of this course:– Philosophy of state variable modelling – Analysis of linear time invariant systems using state space models – Concept of controllability, observability – Merits of state feedback controllers by pole placement – State estimation using both observer and Kalman filter – Linear Quadratic Gaussian (LQG) approach to controller design – Importance of discrete time controllers – Mathematical tools such as z-transform and inverse z-transform which are needed for analysis and design of discrete time controllers. – Stability of discrete time system – Controller design in discrete domain – Important features of nonlinear systems – Singular points of nonlinear systems – Prediction of limit cycle oscillations – Bifurcation phenomenon of nonlinear systems","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 36 hours of lectures, a 10 hour tutorial, 50 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 54 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BE(Hons) - Bachelor of Engineering (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程的主要内容： – 状态变量建模原理 – 使用状态空间模型分析线性时不变系统 – 可控性、可观测性的概念 – 通过极点配置的状态反馈控制器的优点 – 使用观测器和卡尔曼滤波器进行状态估计 – 控制器设计的线性二次高斯 (LQG) 方法 – 离散时间控制器的重要性 – 离散时间控制器分析和设计所需的数学工具，如 z 变换和逆 z 变换。 – 离散时间系统的稳定性 – 离散域控制器设计 – 非线性系统的重要特征 – 非线性系统的奇异点 – 极限环振荡的预测 – 非线性系统的分岔现象"},{"group":"group1","code":"ELECTENG 726","title":"数字通信","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=ELECTENG%20726","officialTitle":"ELECTENG 726 - Digital Communications","prescription":"Advanced principles and techniques in digital transmission systems: baseband and passband digital systems. Geometric representation of signals: theory of orthonormal signals, correlation demodulators, optimal detector. Digital phase (PSK) and frequency (FSK) modulation. Digital communication systems with noise. Information theory, capacity theorem and applications. Signal and information coding: data compression, digital transmission, error detection and correction, block and convolutional codes. Noise, thermal noise, noise figure. Traffic theory. Digital networks and OSI model.","overview":"The courses ELECTENG 726 and ELECTENG 741 deal with aspects of digital communication systems. ELECTENG 726 is intended for undergraduate students enrolled in the BE(Hons) Part IV, whereas ELECTENG 741 is intended for postgraduate students enrolled in the PGCertEng/MEngSt/ME.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, in a typical week, you can expect 3 hours of lectures and 7 hours of reading and thinking about the content and/or working on assignments and/or laboratories. For the 12 teaching weeks, this totals 120 hours. This leaves a total of 30 hours across the entire semester for independent or supplementary study during breaks.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"BE(Hons) - Bachelor of Engineering (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"课程 ELECTENG 726 和 ELECTENG 741 涉及数字通信系统的各个方面。 ELECTENG 726 适用于就读 BE(Hons) Part IV 的本科生，而 ELECTENG 741 适用于就读 PGCertEng/MEngSt/ME 的研究生。"},{"group":"group1","code":"ELECTENG 732","title":"通信系统","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=ELECTENG%20732","officialTitle":"ELECTENG 732 - Communication Systems","prescription":"Analog AM and FM modulation. Noise in AM and FM systems. AM modulators and demodulators. Coherent and non-coherent receivers. Superheterodyne receivers. Multiplexing: FDM, TDM, CDMA. Pulse modulation. Nyquist theorem; PCM modulation and multiplexing. Baseband digital transmission; optimal filtering; matched filter detection; probability of error. Intersymbol interference, waveform coding and data compression, base-band data transmission. Introduction to digital systems and modulations.","overview":"","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BE(Hons) - Bachelor of Engineering (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":""},{"group":"group1","code":"ELECTENG 733","title":"数字信号处理","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=ELECTENG%20733","officialTitle":"ELECTENG 733 - Digital Signal Processing","prescription":"Analysis and manipulation of discrete-time signals and systems. Spectral representations and analysis using the z-transform, discrete Fourier transform and fast Fourier transform. Introduction to stochastic processes. Hardware systems for processing digital signals.","overview":"Digital signal processing is the enabling technology for the generation, transformation, extraction, and interpretation of digital information. This course is designed to provide insights into these processes from both theoretical and practical perspectives. It aims to foster a thorough understanding of the underlying mathematical and statistical modelling techniques for processing and learning from signals. Selected applications from relevant fields such as speech, image, audio, wireless communication, and control systems are introduced to give context and guide further studies and research directions. The topics covered are packaged into two integrated modules: Module 1 Discrete-Time Signal Processing Signal and system representations: sampling and quantisation, complex exponentials, linear time-invariant systems, discrete-time Fourier transform, z-transform, fast Fourier transform. Digital filter design : FIR filter, IIR filter, windowing, bilinear transform, phase and group delay, filter stability. Module 2 Random Signal Processing Probability concepts: probability measures, probability density function (PDF), cumulative distribution function (CDF), random variables, expected values, functions of random variables, correlation and covariance. Stochastic processes: ensembles, stationarity, ergodicity, power spectral density.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course, and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 3 hours of lectures, a 1-hour tutorial, 2 hours of reading, thinking about the content, and solving prescribed problems, and 4 hours of work on a mixture of assignments and/or laboratories and/or test preparation. For the 12 teaching weeks, this totals 120 hours and leaves 30 hours across the entire semester for independent or supplementary study, including during breaks.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BE(Hons) - Bachelor of Engineering (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"数字信号处理是数字信息生成、转换、提取和解释的支持技术。本课程旨在从理论和实践的角度提供对这些过程的见解。它旨在促进对用于处理和学习信号的基础数学和统计建模技术的透彻理解。介绍了语音、图像、音频、无线通信和控制系统等相关领域的精选应用，以提供背景信息并指导进一步的研究和研究方向。涵盖的主题分为两个集成模块： 模块 1 离散时间信号处理 信号和系统表示：采样和量化、复指数、线性时不变系统、离散时间傅里叶变换、z 变换、快速傅里叶变换。数字滤波器设计：FIR滤波器、IIR滤波器、加窗、双线性变换、相位和群延迟、滤波器稳定性。模块 2 随机信号处理概率概念：概率度量、概率密度函数 (PDF)、累积分布函数 (CDF)、随机变量、期望值、随机变量函数、相关性和协方差。随机过程：系综、平稳性、遍历性、功率谱密度。"},{"group":"group1","code":"INFOSYS 722","title":"数据挖掘与大数据","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20722","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 722 - Data Mining and Big Data","prescription":"Data mining and big data involves storing, processing, analysing and making sense of huge volumes of data extracted in many formats and from many sources. Using information systems frameworks and knowledge discovery concepts, this project-based course uses cutting-edge business intelligence tools for data analytics.","overview":"The goals of the course are to introduce students to several foundational concepts which include: Decision Making, Artificial Intelligence (AI), Big Data, Machine Learning, Data Mining, and Generative AI. In addition, students will learn about Big Data and Data Mining Computing Environment – hardware, software, distributed systems, and analytical tools. Students will apply methodologies, processes, algorithms, and approaches for big data analytics to gather insights that deliver value from data. You will also discover through a study of Big Data and Data Mining in Practice how the world’s most successful companies use big data analytics to deliver extraordinary results. This course is applied so you will apply the knowledge gained through the design and implementation of a prototype through multiple iterations. This course is ideal for students of business, science, and engineering.Possible careers include data scientist, data analyst, decision scientist, and business intelligence analyst.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 36 hours of lectures, 24 hours of tutorials, 30 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 60 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"BCom(Hons) - Bachelor of Commerce (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"该课程的目标是向学生介绍几个基本概念，包括：决策、人工智能 (AI)、大数据、机器学习、数据挖掘和生成人工智能。此外，学生还将学习大数据和数据挖掘计算环境——硬件、软件、分布式系统和分析工具。学生将应用大数据分析的方法、流程、算法和方法来收集可从数据中创造价值的见解。您还将通过对大数据和数据挖掘实践的研究，发现世界上最成功的公司如何利用大数据分析来提供非凡的成果。本课程是应用性的，因此您将通过多次迭代应用通过设计和实现原型所获得的知识。本课程非常适合商科、科学和工程专业的学生。可能的职业包括数据科学家、数据分析师、决策科学家和商业智能分析师。"},{"group":"group1","code":"INFOSYS 727","title":"高级信息安全","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20727","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 727 - Advanced Information Security","prescription":"Focuses on technical security issues of the systems used in today's information technology applications. Explores the practical issues of identification and authentication, security of operating systems, cryptography, disaster recovery and contingency planning, and discusses the relevant theoretical models. Managerial aspects of information security issues as well as legal and ethical issues arising from protecting computer files both from a New Zealand and global perspective will be addressed. The course follows the content of CISSP certification.","overview":"This course is for students who want to expand their knowledge of computers, networks, and business security. The course structure is designated to correspond with the domains of knowledge for the CISSP certification, called the Common Body of Knowledge. An important part of the course is the security laboratory presenting various tools used to secure the functioning of information systems. This course is the next logical step after gaining basic knowledge related to using information systems in business. After graduation from this course, the students will be able to effectively use their information technology tools to mitigate the risk of being exposed to possible attacks against their IT systems.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course, and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 36 hours of lectures and tutorials, 64 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 50 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation. The key to successfully completing the course and reducing stress is a regular study program during the whole semester. Lectures will be recorded.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BCom(Hons) - Bachelor of Commerce (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程适合想要扩展计算机、网络和商业安全知识的学生。课程结构被指定为与 CISSP 认证的知识领域（称为公共知识体系）相对应。该课程的一个重要部分是安全实验室，展示用于保护信息系统运行的各种工具。本课程是获得与在商业中使用信息系统相关的基础知识后的下一个逻辑步骤。完成本课程后，学生将能够有效地使用他们的信息技术工具来降低 IT 系统遭受可能攻击的风险。"},{"group":"group1","code":"INFOSYS 735","title":"云计算架构","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20735","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 735 - Cloud Computing Architecture","prescription":"Cloud Computing Architecture combines practical skills development with broader research and critical thinking skills to enable the student to analyze concepts relating to cloud computing. The curriculum is delivered through instructor-led classes, knowledge assessments, and hands-on labs designed to develop technical expertise in cloud computing and preparing students for a career in cloud solutions.","overview":"Cloud Computing Architecture is designed to help students develop technical expertise in cloud computing and prepare them for the AWS Certified Solutions Architect–Associate certification exam. The course validates students' abilities to effectively demonstrate knowledge of how to architect and deploy secure and robust applications on the cloud, defining solutions using architectural design principles and best practices on implementation and life cycle of a project in the cloud","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. During a typical teaching week there will be 3 hours of lectures, and 2 hours of lab workshops. For the 12 teaching weeks, this totals to 60 hours. Since the course as a whole represents approximately 150 hours of study, that leaves a total of 90 hours across the entire semester for independent study, e.g. reading, reflection, preparing for assessments/exams, etc.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BCom(Hons) - Bachelor of Commerce (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"云计算架构旨在帮助学生培养云计算方面的技术专业知识，并为他们准备 AWS Certified Solutions Architect-Associate 认证考试。该课程验证学生有效展示如何在云上构建和部署安全、强大的应用程序的知识，使用架构设计原则和云中项目实施和生命周期的最佳实践定义解决方案的能力"},{"group":"group1","code":"STATS 705","title":"官方统计主题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=STATS%20705","officialTitle":"STATS 705 - Topics in Official Statistics","prescription":"Official statistics, data access, data quality, demographic and health statistics, other social statistics, economic statistics, analysis and presentation, case studies in the use of official statistics.","overview":"STATS 705 describes the Official Statistics system in New Zealand, the uses to which official statistics are put, and discusses legal, ethical, and analytic issues in relation to the use of official statistics. The skills developed in this course are particularly useful for those wishing to have a career in government or business in which an understanding of official statistics (of New Zealand, especially) is an asset. Students undertaking this course will gain a better understanding of New Zealand society and how it is measured. Please note that this course does require a high level of English language proficiency.","topics":"Administrative data & Official Statistics System Demography Health Statistics Legal & Ethical Framework Record Linkage Data Visualisation and GIS Macroeconomics Environmental Statistics Time Series","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 12.5 hours per week involved in each 15 point course in which they are enrolled. For this course, a typical weekly workload includes: 2 hours of lectures 3-5 hours of reviewing the course content 5-7 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MSc - Master of Science","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"STATS 705 描述了新西兰的官方统计系统、官方统计数据的用途，并讨论了与官方统计数据使用相关的法律、道德和分析问题。本课程中培养的技能对于那些希望在政府或企业从事职业的人特别有用，了解官方统计数据（尤其是新西兰）是一项资产。学习本课程的学生将更好地了解新西兰社会及其衡量方式。请注意，本课程确实需要高水平的英语语言能力。"},{"group":"group1","code":"STATS 707","title":"统计计算导论","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=STATS%20707","officialTitle":"STATS 707 - Computational Introduction to Statistics","prescription":"An advanced introduction to statistics and data analysis, including testing, estimation and linear regression.","overview":"STATS 707 is a systematic introduction to statistics and data analysis aimed at graduate students from other disciplines who have some computing experience. The course aims to cover the basic toolkit of statistical data analysis as well as an introduction to concepts and philosophical underpinnings of statistical inference, both frequentist and Bayesian.","topics":"Introduction to Probability Introduction to mathematical statistics and statistical inference: point and interval estimation, hypothesis testing, ANOVA. Introduction to linear/multiple linear regression and generalized linear regression.","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 150 hours over the course of the semester involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, a typical weekly workload (on-an average) includes: 4 hours of lectures (including in-class workshops/quizzes) 3 hours of reviewing the course content 3-4 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BSc(Hons) - Bachelor of Science (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"STATS 707 是对统计和数据分析的系统介绍，面向具有一定计算经验的其他学科的研究生。本课程旨在涵盖统计数据分析的基本工具包，以及统计推断（频率论和贝叶斯论）的概念和哲学基础的介绍。"},{"group":"group1","code":"STATS 762","title":"数据科学回归","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=STATS%20762","officialTitle":"STATS 762 - Regression for Data Science","prescription":"Application of the generalised linear model to fit data arising from a wide range of sources, including multiple linear regression models, Poisson regression, and logistic regression models. The graphical exploration of data. Model building for prediction and for causal inference. Other regression models such as quantile regression. A basic understanding of vector spaces, matrix algebra and calculus will be assumed.","overview":"The main emphasis of this course is on analysing data using the regression methods introduced in STATS 201/208 and their extensions. The practical aspects of fitting linear and generalised linear models are reviewed including estimation, diagnostics and inference. The geometric interpretation of the linear model is presented to enhance the understanding of these models. Simulation-based procedures, including bootstrapping and cross-validation, are introduced as a means to provide robust inference and to investigate the consequences of assumption violations. The two main uses of regression models, prediction and explanation, are discussed. Issues related to predictive models, including ways to estimate prediction error, model selection criteria, model building, and ethics of predictive modelling are discussed. Issues related to explanatory models including confounding, the model choice for causal inference and causal graphs are explored. Other modern regression methods such as lasso and quantile regression are introduced. Emphasis is on practical application, providing students with a versatile statistical toolbox useful for a range of fields in both academia and industry, including applied statistics, data science, and almost all subjects in Business and Economics, along with any experimental or social science.","topics":"Generalised Linear Models Generalised Additive Models Model Selection Simulation Bootstrapping Predictive models Explanatory models Regression with penalty Classification and regression trees Splines Gaussian process","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, a typical weekly workload includes: 3 hours of lectures 1-hour tutorial 3 hours of reviewing the course content 3 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MSc - Master of Science","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程的主要重点是使用 STATS 201/208 及其扩展中介绍的回归方法来分析数据。回顾了拟合线性和广义线性模型的实际方面，包括估计、诊断和推理。提出线性模型的几何解释是为了增强对这些模型的理解。引入基于模拟的程序，包括引导和交叉验证，作为提供稳健推理和调查假设违反的后果的手段。讨论了回归模型的两个主要用途：预测和解释。讨论了与预测模型相关的问题，包括估计预测误差的方法、模型选择标准、模型构建和预测建模的伦理。探讨了与解释模型相关的问题，包括混杂、因果推理的模型选择和因果图。介绍了其他现代回归方法，例如套索回归和分位数回归。重点是实际应用，为学生提供一个多功能的统计工具箱，可用于学术界和工业界的一系列领域，包括应用统计、数据科学、商业和经济学的几乎所有科目，以及任何实验或社会科学。"},{"group":"group2","code":"DIGIHLTH 701","title":"数字健康原理","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=DIGIHLTH%20701","officialTitle":"DIGIHLTH 701 - Principles of Digital Health","prescription":"The study of information technology and information management concepts relevant to the delivery of high quality and cost-effective healthcare. Theoretical frameworks such as data management, decision support, strategic planning and implementation, change management, knowledge management and privacy and other ethical aspects of digital health are included.","overview":"In this course we examine the underlying principles, theory and practice that influence the planning, development, delivery and evaluation of information and technology in healthcare. The course materials are all online. We cover the following material: Definition and nature of digital health, and its role in improving health outcomes The ethics of digital health, e.g. privacy, confidentiality and security Relevance of digital health to the delivery of healthcare in New Zealand and globally, including strategic imperatives, governance, implementation and evaluation, and how digital health principles are applied in other countries Exploration of the role of information and technology in healthcare delivery.","topics":"","workload":"The course is a standard 15-point course. Students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course they are enrolled in.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"PGDipHSc - Postgraduate Diploma in Health Sciences","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"在本课程中，我们研究影响医疗保健信息和技术的规划、开发、交付和评估的基本原则、理论和实践。课程材料全部在线。我们涵盖以下材料： 数字健康的定义和性质，及其在改善健康结果中的作用 数字健康的伦理，例如隐私、保密性和安全性 数字健康与新西兰和全球医疗保健提供的相关性，包括战略要务、治理、实施和评估，以及数字健康原则如何在其他国家应用 探索信息和技术在医疗保健提供中的作用。"},{"group":"group2","code":"DIGIHLTH 702","title":"健康知识管理","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=DIGIHLTH%20702","officialTitle":"DIGIHLTH 702 - Health Knowledge Management","prescription":"Analyses the role and dynamics of knowledge in the working environment in the health sector, and develops aspects of knowledge infrastructure.","overview":"The course is structured into three sections. \"Establishing context\" sets the scene for your learning, and involves getting to know one another, learning about knowledge management concepts and paradigms, and establishing your preferred topic for your three assignments. This is preparation for assignment 1. \"Linking your topic to knowledge management concepts\" takes a look at the role of technology in knowledge management, and associated innovation and change management. In this section you link the literature about knowledge management to your topic. This is preparation for assignment 2. \"Your topic as knowledge management\" takes a look at communities of practice, knowledge sharing, governance and strategy, and other knowledge management concepts, and we look to the future of knowledge management. This is preparation for assignment 3. In this way the course aims to equip you to apply knowledge management concepts in the healthcare workplace to enhance the performance of our health system.","topics":"","workload":"The course is a standard 15-point course. Students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course they are enrolled in.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"PGDipHSc - Postgraduate Diploma in Health Sciences","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程分为三个部分。 “建立背景”为您的学习设定场景，包括相互了解、学习知识管理概念和范式，以及为您的三项作业建立您喜欢的主题。这是作业 1 的准备。“将您的主题与知识管理概念联系起来”着眼于技术在知识管理以及相关创新和变革管理中的作用。在本节中，您将有关知识管理的文献链接到您的主题。这是作业 2 的准备。“您的主题是知识管理”着眼于实践社区、知识共享、治理和战略以及其他知识管理概念，我们展望了知识管理的未来。这是作业 3 的准备。通过这种方式，本课程旨在帮助您在医疗保健工作场所应用知识管理概念，以提高我们卫生系统的绩效。"},{"group":"group2","code":"DIGIHLTH 703","title":"新西兰健康数据景观","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=DIGIHLTH%20703","officialTitle":"DIGIHLTH 703 - New Zealand Health Data Landscape","prescription":"An overview of key issues to support the appropriate and effective use of large volumes of routinely collected data to drive improvements in the delivery of health care. Ethical and equitable use of health data, critical evaluation of health data, identification of analytic methods and appropriate interpretation to support health care decision-making are discussed. Specific datasets are not analysed.","overview":"Clinicians, health service managers and analysts, along with researchers, are able to access an increasing array of routinely collected repositories of “big data” from health and other sectors that can be linked using New Zealand’s unique national identifier – the National Health Index number. This health data landscape provides tremendous opportunities for improving the delivery of population and personalised health care and providing efficient mechanisms for undertaking research on a large scale. Along with this potential though are significant pitfalls that need to be considered in order to optimise the use of such data and avoid its misuse. This course will help you to optimize decision-making based on routinely collected New Zealand health data, and to enhance the potential for these data to support equitable population health outcomes. This will be achieved by developing your ability to evaluate the strengths and potential benefits of analyses and outputs using routinely collected health data, as well as their limitations and potential harms.","topics":"","workload":"The course is a standard 15-point course. Students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course they are enrolled in.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"PGDipHSc - Postgraduate Diploma in Health Sciences","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"临床医生、卫生服务管理人员和分析师以及研究人员能够访问越来越多的来自卫生和其他部门的定期收集的“大数据”存储库，这些存储库可以使用新西兰独特的国家标识符（国家健康指数编号）进行链接。这种健康数据格局为改善人口和个性化医疗保健的提供提供了巨大的机会，并为开展大规模研究提供了有效的机制。然而，除了这种潜力之外，还需要考虑一些重大缺陷，以便优化此类数据的使用并避免其滥用。本课程将帮助您根据定期收集的新西兰健康数据优化决策，并增强这些数据支持公平人口健康结果的潜力。这将通过培养您使用常规收集的健康数据评估分析和输出的优势和潜在益处及其局限性和潜在危害的能力来实现。"},{"group":"group2","code":"DIGIHLTH 704","title":"医疗中的人工智能","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=DIGIHLTH%20704","officialTitle":"DIGIHLTH 704 - Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare","prescription":"Familiarises students with the main developments and applications of artificial intelligence in healthcare. The theoretical concepts and the technology including predictive and generative artificial intelligence are outlined. Governance issues are also addressed.","overview":"Artificial intelligence (AI)-based digital health is being developed and implemented at pace. The focus of this course is on understanding the main developments and applications of AI to the health care sector, and how to realise the tremendous potential of AI to improve healthcare delivery and outcomes in Aotearoa. The teaching team will draw on expertise from across the health sector.","topics":"","workload":"The course is a standard 15-point course. Students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course they are enrolled in.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"PGDipHSc - Postgraduate Diploma in Health Sciences","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"基于人工智能（AI）的数字医疗正在快速开发和实施。本课程的重点是了解人工智能在医疗保健领域的主要发展和应用，以及如何发挥人工智能的巨大潜力来改善新西兰的医疗保健服务和结果。教学团队将利用整个卫生部门的专业知识。"},{"group":"group2","code":"DIGIHLTH 705","title":"数字健康设计与评估","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=DIGIHLTH%20705","officialTitle":"DIGIHLTH 705 - Digital Health Design and Evaluation","prescription":"Examines the design and development of digital health tools to meet end-user and health service needs. A series of case studies are used to illustrate the different stages of digital health tool development, evaluation and implementation. Health service, researcher and end-user perspectives are covered.","overview":"The objective of the course is to develop an evidence-based understanding of the principles, frameworks and methods used to design, develop, evaluate and implement digital health tools. This course is structured into five parts covering different areas of digital health design and evaluation. Throughout each part case studies of digital tools are presented: Part 1 covers the consultation and formative work needed to inform the design and development of digital tools Part 2 covers intervention design and development Part 3 covers evaluation of digital tools Part 4 covers implementation including key considerations and discussion of the barriers that might be encountered Part 5 covers important considerations including individual and system considerations as well as equity and ethics.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend at least 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. Each week's 10 hours should consist of Listening to recorded lectures/interviews/discussions/other resources Reading at least the required readings that have been listed. Optional readings are also provided to broaden learning. Reading widely will improve your learning experience and your assignment performance, so find extra readings that interest you and share them online with others in the course Organising and preparing for your assignments. The sooner you start the assignment preparation the better Allocate some time each week for the online discussion.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"PGDipHSc - Postgraduate Diploma in Health Sciences","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"该课程的目标是对用于设计、开发、评估和实施数字健康工具的原则、框架和方法形成基于证据的理解。本课程分为五个部分，涵盖数字健康设计和评估的不同领域。每个部分都介绍了数字工具的案例研究： 第 1 部分涵盖为数字工具的设计和开发提供信息所需的咨询和形成性工作 第 2 部分涵盖干预设计和开发 第 3 部分涵盖数字工具的评估 第 4 部分涵盖实施，包括关键考虑因素和可能遇到的障碍的讨论 第 5 部分涵盖重要考虑因素，包括个人和系统考虑因素以及公平和道德。"},{"group":"group2","code":"DIGIHLTH 706","title":"健康数据分析","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=DIGIHLTH%20706","officialTitle":"DIGIHLTH 706 - Health Data Analytics","prescription":"Analyses, interprets, and presents quantitative data to assist decision making in the health sector. Fundamental elements of statistics, data management, visualisation, epidemiology and computing are covered.","overview":"The objective of this course is to develop skills and confidence in extracting meaning from data. Health practice and research both inform and are informed by the evidence base, much of which is represented by quantitative summaries, figures, models and statistical tests. It is important to be able to critically evaluate existing evidence for it's relevance to a current situation yet the skills or knowledge needed to do that aren't always included in our professional training. This course will place emphasis on interpreting the results of statistical summaries and tests in the context of health and the health system. In addition to summary statistics, data visualisation, correlation and model coefficients, we will investigate just what statistical significance does and doesn't mean. We will then move on to the pros and cons of categorising continuous measurements, the development and use of reference values, and the key things to think about in a meta-analysis. A plethora of tools, devices, algorithms, and scores exist in health, with more added every day, so we will introduce how to assess their performance, enabling critical evaluation of their suitability to a given situation. Throughout, we will use iNZight software to support your learning. You do not need to have used statistical software before.","topics":"","workload":"The course is a standard 15-point course. Students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course they are enrolled in.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"PGDipHSc - Postgraduate Diploma in Health Sciences","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程的目标是培养从数据中提取含义的技能和信心。健康实践和研究既提供证据又受证据基础的影响，其中大部分以定量总结、数字、模型和统计测试为代表。能够批判性地评估现有证据与当前情况的相关性非常重要，但我们的专业培训并不总是包含这样做所需的技能或知识。本课程将侧重于解释健康和卫生系统背景下的统计摘要和测试结果。除了汇总统计、数据可视化、相关性和模型系数之外，我们还将研究统计显着性意味着什么和不意味着什么。然后，我们将继续讨论连续测量分类的优缺点、参考值的开发和使用以及荟萃分析中需要考虑的关键事项。健康领域存在大量的工具、设备、算法和分数，而且每天都会有更多的工具、设备、算法和分数，因此我们将介绍如何评估它们的性能，从而能够对它们对特定情况的适用性进行批判性评估。 Throughout, we will use iNZight software to support your learning.您以前不需要使用过统计软件。"},{"group":"group2","code":"GLMI 701","title":"全球竞争","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=GLMI%20701","officialTitle":"GLMI 701 - Competing Globally","prescription":"Examines why, when and how firms compete across borders, by analysing economic, social, institutional and evolutionary perspectives of what makes some firms more successful than their competitors. Emphasis on the dynamics of contemporary global markets and how international businesses both create and adapt to industry changes.","overview":"This is an interactive, seminar-based course which aims to provide students with a set of theories, concepts and techniques to develop their skills of in-depth analysis and assessment of competition in diverse industrial fields and locations. We address the challenges of applying frameworks and analysing competition in an international context. In addition to readings and case studies, we work with examples that relate to the interests of the students and their intended future research and/or careers.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 3 hours of class time per week that mix lectures, presentations, team-based learning and one-on-one interactions, 114 hours of reading and thinking about the content and working on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"这是一门以研讨会为基础的互动课程，旨在为学生提供一套理论、概念和技术，以培养他们对不同工业领域和地点的竞争进行深入分析和评估的技能。我们解决在国际背景下应用框架和分析竞争的挑战。除了阅读材料和案例研究之外，我们还提供与学生的兴趣及其未来预期研究和/或职业相关的示例。"},{"group":"group2","code":"GLMI 703","title":"全球数字战略","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=GLMI%20703","officialTitle":"GLMI 703 - Global Digital Strategy - Level 9","prescription":"Critically evaluates digital strategies in global markets, focusing on digital transformations and strategic innovations in multinational and born-digital firms. Assesses and creates strategic solutions for digital challenges in international business, emphasising digital platforms, cross-border alliances and global sustainability.","overview":"Critically evaluates digital strategies in global markets, focusing on digital transformations and strategic innovations in multinational and born-digital firms. Assesses and creates strategic solutions for digital challenges in international business, emphasising digital platforms, cross-border alliances and global sustainability.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 3 hours of lectures, 3.5 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 3.5 hours of work on assignments and group project.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"批判性地评估全球市场的数字战略，重点关注跨国公司和天生数字化公司的数字化转型和战略创新。评估国际业务中的数字挑战并制定战略解决方案，强调数字平台、跨境联盟和全球可持续发展。"},{"group":"group2","code":"GLMI 704","title":"全球可持续发展","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=GLMI%20704","officialTitle":"GLMI 704 - Global Sustainability","prescription":"Investigates social, environmental and governance challenges facing businesses and societies and explores the sustainability of corporate strategies in global markets. Considers the role of competition and governance in shaping international business responses to sustainability challenges, solutions and potential pathways towards greater sustainability.","overview":"What determines business sustainability in a world in which markets are exposed to global forces? To what extent are problems such as climate change, inequality or labour exploitation either aggravated or eased by international trade and investment linkages? This course investigates the sustainability implications of increased global integration of markets, the underlying causes of market and regulatory failure, and some of the central challenges associated with these processes. We investigate the effects for particular firms, industries and other stakeholders. We also examine the ways businesses, governments and societal groups have responded to calls for more sustainable business practices. The course is based on interactive, seminar-style classes as well as independent reading and group activity.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 3 hours of seminar, 3 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 4 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"在市场面临全球力量的世界中，什么决定着企业的可持续性？国际贸易和投资联系在多大程度上加剧或缓解了气候变化、不平等或劳动力剥削等问题？本课程研究全球市场一体化程度加深对可持续性的影响、市场和监管失败的根本原因，以及与这些过程相关的一些核心挑战。我们调查对特定公司、行业和其他利益相关者的影响。我们还研究了企业、政府和社会团体响应更可持续商业实践呼吁的方式。该课程以互动、研讨会式的课程以及独立阅读和小组活动为基础。"},{"group":"group2","code":"GLMI 705","title":"人、表现与幸福感","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=GLMI%20705","officialTitle":"GLMI 705 - People, Performance and Well-being - Level 9","prescription":"Evaluates the dynamics of the employment relationship, emphasising the critical analysis of how human resource strategies influence organisational performance and employee well-being. Examines approaches used to enhance mutuality and motivation within workforces, focusing on strategic development and the balance of organisational, societal and employee needs.","overview":"This course is about understanding how employee wellbeing influences performance and quality of work life. You will develop a critical appreciation of theory and research in this area while also building skills in how to analyse complexities and problems in enhancing the quality of working life. This is intended to enhance your ability to improve the quality of well-being for yourself and for others you work with (for example, when you occupy a management or an HR specialist role).","topics":"Wellbeing - Macro and Micro theories; Performance; Leadership; Psychological Safety","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程旨在了解员工福祉如何影响绩效和工作生活质量。您将对这一领域的理论和研究进行批判性的评价，同时培养如何分析复杂性和问题以提高工作生活质量的技能。这样做的目的是增强您提高自己和同事的福祉质量的能力（例如，当您担任管理或人力资源专家角色时）。"},{"group":"group2","code":"GLMI 706","title":"不确定时代的工作","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=GLMI%20706","officialTitle":"GLMI 706 - Working in an Age of Uncertainty","prescription":"Explores the contemporary environment which contains high levels of uncertainty, stemming from new technologies and changes in economy and society. Critically examines issues confronting organisations and work in these fast-paced, fluid and complex contexts, such as power and voice, meaning and dignity, and alternative forms of organising.","overview":"This course is designed for students seeking to expand and deepen their critical understanding of work and organisations. The course content is inspired by three main questions: • What factors are affecting continuity and change at work? • What are the personal consequences of contemporary work systems? • How can we respond, individually and collectively, to the challenges and opportunities of contemporary conditions? We will investigate these questions by using theory and research from social psychology, sociology of work, industrial relations, organisation studies and HRM. Our class will meet for three hours, once a week, over the course of the semester. These classes will be discussion based so students will need to come to class prepared to contribute. We will debate, analyse, and question theory, topical cases, and our own assumptions and experiences.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程专为寻求扩展和加深对工作和组织的批判性理解的学生而设计。课程内容的灵感来源于三个主要问题： • 哪些因素影响工作的连续性和变化？ • 当代工作制度对个人有何影响？ • 我们如何以个人和集体的方式应对当代条件下的挑战和机遇？我们将利用社会心理学、工作社会学、劳资关系、组织研究和人力资源管理的理论和研究来调查这些问题。在整个学期中，我们的班级每周一次，每次三个小时。这些课程将以讨论为基础，因此学生需要在课堂上做好贡献的准备。我们将辩论、分析和质疑理论、热门案例以及我们自己的假设和经验。"},{"group":"group2","code":"GLMI 707","title":"组织变革","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=GLMI%20707","officialTitle":"GLMI 707 - Organisational Change","prescription":"Explores the field of organisational change and explains its relevance to organisational outcomes. Considers different organisational change models and assess their application to specific organisational issues. Develops skills related to effective diagnosis and management of organisational change and in determining its impact on key stakeholders, including employees.","overview":"Leaders today are charged with developing and growing organisations within constantly shifting volatility, uncertainty, complexity and ambiguity (VUCA) world of work. Leading change today, means that past solutions cannot be relied on to mitigate current or future change encounters. Now organisational change requires leaders to have proactive mindsets and influencing skills that gain traction for changes that may not have even occurred yet. This course is designed to challenge how you as leaders think, react, and ultimately influence others, within constant change and ambiguity. Be prepared to do things differently in this course; ensure you check the timetable for class times – these are not your usual timetables; be prepared to engage in experiential learning, group debates and discussions, personal reflection, real life examples, and leadership scenarios that will challenge your view of the world of work, leadership and change.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 6 hours of seminars, 6 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 8 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"当今的领导者负责在不断变化的波动性、不确定性、复杂性和模糊性 (VUCA) 工作世界中发展和壮大组织。今天引领变革意味着不能依赖过去的解决方案来缓解当前或未来的变革遭遇。现在，组织变革要求领导者拥有积极主动的心态和影响力技能，从而为可能尚未发生的变革赢得动力。本课程旨在挑战领导者在不断变化和模糊的情况下如何思考、反应并最终影响他人。准备好在本课程中以不同的方式做事；确保您查看上课时间的时间表——这些不是您平常的时间表；准备好参与体验式学习、小组辩论和讨论、个人反思、现实生活中的例子和领导力场景，这些将挑战您对工作世界、领导力和变革的看法。"},{"group":"group2","code":"GLMI 708","title":"自我领导与职业成长","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=GLMI%20708","officialTitle":"GLMI 708 - Self-leadership and Professional Growth","prescription":"Focuses on knowledge of theory, self-directed explorations of values and skill development. Provides opportunities to master techniques in self-management and self-leadership. Develops professional capabilities which are applied to a project.","overview":"","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":""},{"group":"group2","code":"GLMI 709","title":"新兴创业与全球互联互通","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=GLMI%20709","officialTitle":"GLMI 709 - New Ventures and Global Connectivity","prescription":"Explores the complexities of launching new ventures in a connected world. Focuses on evaluating opportunities and impact, formulating business models, and strategies for mobilising knowledge and resources. Highlights the significance of trends like digitalisation and sustainability within and between entrepreneurial ecosystems.","overview":"The course alerts students to key challenges and strategic decisions that will be faced in developing a new global venture. Participants learn how to identify and evaluate opportunities, and use frameworks and tools to evaluate risk, apply strategic thinking and develop a viable business model. Transferable skills related to teamwork, problem solving, and communication are developed by working in a team to move a business idea toward reality. This elective course can contribute 15 points towards the MCom, BCom Hons, PGDip, and several degree programmes offered outside of the business school, including the Master of Information Technology.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 30 hours of lectures, 50 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 70 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"该课程提醒学生在开发新的全球企业时将面临的关键挑战和战略决策。参与者学习如何识别和评估机会，并使用框架和工具来评估风险，应用战略思维并开发可行的商业模式。与团队合作、解决问题和沟通相关的可转移技能是通过在团队中工作将商业想法变为现实来发展的。该选修课程可为 MCom、BCom Hons、PGDip 以及商学院以外提供的多个学位课程（包括信息技术硕士）贡献 15 分。"},{"group":"group2","code":"GLMI 710","title":"利用创新生态系统","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=GLMI%20710","officialTitle":"GLMI 710 - Leveraging Innovation Ecosystems","prescription":"Considers the role of knowledge in supporting innovation for organisational profitability and growth, including core knowledge processes and approaches for managing knowledge in transdisciplinary contexts. Explores effective strategies on managing knowledge processes to support cooperation and collaboration among diverse stakeholders across transdisciplinary contexts.","overview":"For thirty-plus years, scholars, consultants, and senior executives have recognised knowledge as a critical dimension in the innovation process for any organisation. Yet, effective knowledge management of innovation remains a perennial challenge for most organisations. Knowledge can be conceptualised in different ways: possessive, processual, and indigenous views and their assumptions for managing knowledge processes for innovation are introduced in this course. Grappling with knowledge processes for innovation, such as creation, transfer and orchestration, is even more important in the context of emerging technologies like artificial intelligence, machine learning, and nanotechnology, whose development or practical applications (or both) are still largely unrealised.This course aims to: Provide seminal frameworks to understand knowledge processes for innovation that are grounded in the academic disciplines of knowledge management and innovation management Use those frameworks to explore what knowledge processes can be managed in the face of emerging technologies, which present opportunities and threats to innovation in several organisational contexts.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course, and students are expected to spend 12-15 hours per week in each 15-point course they are enrolled in. For this course, students can expect one 3-hour class per week. There, we clarify key concepts in the pre-set readings and discuss them with regard to contemporary situations that students are working on and the lecturers prescribe. There are assignment Q&A sessions some weeks. Weeks 11 and 12 are dedicated to completing the 50% written assignment and the lecturer is available for 1:1 and small group meetings. Outside classes, students are expected to spend up to 5 hours each week preparing for class and 5 hours working on assignments.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"三十多年来，学者、顾问和高级管理人员已经认识到知识是任何组织创新过程中的一个关键维度。然而，有效的创新知识管理仍然是大多数组织面临的长期挑战。知识可以用不同的方式概念化：本课程介绍了所有格、过程和本土观点及其管理创新知识过程的假设。在人工智能、机器学习和纳米技术等新兴技术的背景下，解决创新知识流程（例如创造、转移和编排）显得尤为重要，这些技术的开发或实际应用（或两者）在很大程度上尚未实现。本课程旨在： 提供开创性框架，以理解基于知识管理和创新管理学科的创新知识流程 使用这些框架来探索面对新兴技术可以管理哪些知识流程，这些技术为多个组织的创新带来了机会和威胁上下文。"},{"group":"group2","code":"GLMI 711","title":"战略创业实践","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=GLMI%20711","officialTitle":"GLMI 711 - Strategic Entrepreneurship in Action","prescription":"Examines the challenges of planning and decision-making in the unpredictable world of startups and new ventures. Equips students with theoretical insights and practical tools for strategic analysis and management to enable the crafting of effective strategies in real-world entrepreneurial scenarios. Uses case studies and applied projects to explore the strategies that drive success in innovative and fast-paced business environments.","overview":"GLMI 711 is a part of the MCom in Global Management and Innovation programme, although this course can be taken as an elective by students from other Masters programmes (Engineering, Psychology, Biotechnology, IT). The course will introduce you to a selection of concepts and frameworks related to Strategic Entrepreneurship, Innovation, and Digital Transformation. It explores how contemporary entrepreneurial businesses (small and large) differentiate, compete, and create value. The course focuses on how entrepreneurial businesses build upon and manage their resources and engage with key partners in ways that leverage entrepreneurial action and approaches. GLMI 711 discusses important intersections between innovation, firm capabilities, and the dynamics of entrepreneurial mindsets. The aim is to offer a rich understanding of how organisations identify, exploit, and shape technological opportunities and the markets they seek to enter as well as how such opportunities can be influenced by the context.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. We will have 2 hours per week of in-class sessions, as well as significant delivery of content, including articles, videos and podcasts, and interactive tools via the Canvas platform. The course relies on open and inclusive discussions to encourage critical thinking and learning. Instead of traditional lectures, the class uses a mix of in-class discussions, students’ presentations, group activities and guest sessions. As we are dealing with complex topics, there are no simple, correct answers and, most of the time, you will need to build and support your argument. Hence, it is paramount that you come to the class prepared each week. You are expected to complete and engage with the readings. You are not expected to memorise but, instead, to understand and demonstrate the ability to apply ideas covered in the course. The final assignment is based on a current issue from a real-life organisation: this means you would need to get to know the case organisation and the sector, relate the class to current news and events and you would be encouraged to draw on other disciplinary approaches and frameworks as well as your own personal experience. Each week you will be required to cover 2-3 academic and practitioner readings, with a heavier reading requirement in the first part of the semester and more individual work in the second part of the semester.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"GLMI 711 是 MCom 全球管理和创新课程的一部分，尽管其他硕士课程（工程、心理学、生物技术、IT）的学生可以将本课程作为选修课。本课程将向您介绍与战略创业、创新和数字化转型相关的一系列概念和框架。它探讨了当代创业型企业（小型和大型）如何差异化、竞争和创造价值。该课程重点关注创业企业如何利用和管理其资源，以及如何利用创业行动和方法与关键合作伙伴互动。 GLMI 711 讨论了创新、公司能力和创业思维动态之间的重要交叉点。目的是让人们深入了解组织如何识别、利用和塑造技术机会和他们寻求进入的市场，以及这些机会如何受到环境的影响。"},{"group":"group2","code":"GLMI 712","title":"当代创新与商业设计方法","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=GLMI%20712","officialTitle":"GLMI 712 - Contemporary Approaches to Innovation and Business Design","prescription":"Develops understanding of design tools and processes essential for fostering innovation in businesses and organisations. Through theoretical knowledge and practical application, students explore design methodologies, tools and techniques used to drive forward-thinking solutions.","overview":"This course offers a unique opportunity for students to explore the intersection of serendipity-driven innovation and entrepreneurship in addressing a complex social and environmental problem through creative design thinking techniques. Students will engage in a series of interactive sessions, supported by curated readings, to develop sustainable business ideas for real-world problems while incorporating serendipity into their ideation process. We take a step-by-step approach: foster problem recognition for sustainability issues, experience serendipity-driven innovation and ideate an entrepreneurial solution. Whilst studying the relationships between entrepreneurship, innovation, and creativity theoretically, students are expected to apply their insights and approach learning in creative and collaborative ways throughout the Semester. To succeed in this course, students should be open to reading widely from a range of disciplines, willing to discuss ideas with others, and ready to challenge themselves to try new things in an interactive classroom format. The course is targeted at graduate students who are keen to work on a social or environmental problem on a global scale.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 3 hours of lectures, 5 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 5] hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程为学生提供了一个独特的机会，让他们探索偶然驱动的创新与创业精神的交叉点，通过创造性的设计思维技术解决复杂的社会和环境问题。学生将参加一系列互动课程，并辅以精选阅读材料，为现实世界的问题开发可持续的商业创意，同时将偶然性融入到他们的构思过程中。我们采取循序渐进的方法：培养对可持续发展问题的认识，体验偶然性驱动的创新并构思创业解决方案。在从理论上研究创业、创新和创造力之间的关系的同时，学生们应该在整个学期中以创造性和协作的方式运用他们的见解并进行学习。为了在这门课程中取得成功，学生应该愿意广泛阅读各个学科的内容，愿意与他人讨论想法，并准备好挑战自己，以互动课堂形式尝试新事物。该课程针对热衷于解决全球范围内的社会或环境问题的研究生。"},{"group":"group2","code":"INFOSYS 700","title":"数字创新","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20700","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 700 - Digital Innovation","prescription":"New information technologies are transforming how innovations are created, distributed, and commercialised. Focuses on the practices for digital innovation creation, distribution, and commercialisation as well as the digital strategies needed to manage such digital innovations.","overview":"Students will explore the theoretical and practical aspects of digital innovation, their potential impact, and the disruption on business and society. Participants will investigate different aspects of these innovations through a number of mediums: oral, written and practical. By the end of the course, students will have a deep understanding of these topics and its application in the social and business context.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15-point course, and students are expected to spend a minimum of 10 hours per week involved in each 15-point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 3 hours of seminar time, 3 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 4 hours of work on assignments and test preparation. You will be expected to read and review weekly set materials prior to class time and participate in classroom activities.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"学生将探索数字创新的理论和实践方面、其潜在影响以及对商业和社会的颠覆。参与者将通过多种媒介研究这些创新的不同方面：口头、书面和实践。课程结束时，学生将对这些主题及其在社会和商业环境中的应用有深入的了解。"},{"group":"group2","code":"INFOSYS 702","title":"专题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20702","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 702 - Special Topic","prescription":"","overview":"This course is designed for students keen to explore emerging or niche areas. Learning outcomes include advanced knowledge in a specialised subject area, critically evaluating current research and practice, and communicating ideas in written reports and/or oral presentations.","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程专为热衷于探索新兴或利基领域的学生而设计。学习成果包括专业学科领域的先进知识、批判性地评估当前的研究和实践，以及在书面报告和/或口头演示中交流想法。"},{"group":"group2","code":"INFOSYS 703","title":"人工智能管理","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20703","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 703 - Managing with Artificial Intelligence","prescription":"Focuses on a business perspective of the use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools and solutions in organisations. Explores how different AI-enabled tools and solutions contribute to organisational and societal objectives and values. Aims to prepare students for jobs that involve technology consultancy and management of technology, with specific focus on AI as an emergent technology.","overview":"The course will provide a business-focused perspective on the use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools and solutions in organizations, with emphasis on the fundamentals of business and management. It will explore how different AI-enabled tools and solutions contribute to organizational and societal objectives and values. Specifically, in the organizational context the course will focus on how AI could be used to improve operational performance and deliver value to its key stakeholders, and how to manage with AI. It will also discuss the relationships between data and AI tools, to understand potential limitations of AI if relevant data is not available or fragmented. The course will cover constraints and consequences of using AI tools such as data privacy, regulations and ethics; selection, implementation and governance of AI; as well as dependencies that may prohibit integration of AI with other tools and technologies used in the organization. It will expose students to a broad range of applications of AI in various industries and organizations. The course aims to prepare student to jobs that involve technology consultancy and management of technology, with specific focus on AI tools.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 36 hours of lectures (including seminar work), 24 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 90 hours of work on group and individual assignments.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"该课程将从以业务为中心的角度介绍人工智能（AI）工具和解决方案在组织中的使用，重点是业务和管理的基础知识。它将探讨不同的人工智能工具和解决方案如何为组织和社会的目标和价值做出贡献。具体来说，在组织背景下，该课程将重点关注如何使用人工智能来提高运营绩效并为其关键利益相关者提供价值，以及如何使用人工智能进行管理。它还将讨论数据和人工智能工具之间的关系，以了解在相关数据不可用或碎片化的情况下人工智能的潜在局限性。该课程将涵盖使用人工智能工具的限制和后果，例如数据隐私、法规和道德；人工智能的选择、实施和治理；以及可能阻止人工智能与组织中使用的其他工具和技术集成的依赖关系。它将让学生接触到人工智能在各个行业和组织中的广泛应用。该课程旨在帮助学生为涉及技术咨询和技术管理的工作做好准备，特别关注人工智能工具。"},{"group":"group2","code":"INFOSYS 704","title":"IT 咨询","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20704","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 704 - IT Consultancy","prescription":"Delves into IT consulting, equipping students with the skills to analyse, evaluate and provide effective counsel to organisations in the public and private sectors. Students are introduced to concepts, frameworks, processes, roadmaps and technologies of IT consulting. Equips students with the frameworks, processes, roadmaps and technologies to support IT consultancy processes employed in advising organisations in the public and private sectors.","overview":"This course explores the profession of IT consulting and the art and science of providing counsel to organizations in the public and private sectors. The course is designed to provide a framework for collaborating with organizations to solve problems and to execute IT projects efficiently and effectively. Students will explore and utilize practical tools that will enable them to solve problems and execute projects as consultants or as individual contributors within organizations. The course introduces frameworks as well as methods and tools that are typically used in IT consulting.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 24 hours of lectures, 24 hours of lab/ tutorial, 36 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 66 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程探讨 IT 咨询专业以及为公共和私营部门组织提供咨询的艺术和科学。该课程旨在提供一个与组织合作解决问题并高效执行 IT 项目的框架。学生将探索和利用实用工具，使他们能够作为组织内的顾问或个人贡献者解决问题并执行项目。本课程介绍 IT 咨询中通常使用的框架以及方法和工具。"},{"group":"group2","code":"INFOSYS 705","title":"导向学习","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20705","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 705 - Directed Study","prescription":"","overview":"A research-focused course emphasising self-directed work to support the development and application of key academic competencies and research skills. Assessment methods may include peer review, poster or oral presentations, research proposals, and written reports or a thesis.","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"以研究为重点的课程，强调自主工作，以支持关键学术能力和研究技能的发展和应用。评估方法可能包括同行评审、海报或口头演示、研究计划以及书面报告或论文。"},{"group":"group2","code":"INFOSYS 706","title":"数字可持续发展","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20706","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 706 - Digital Sustainability","prescription":"Informs students about the opportunities in harnessing technology to address the UN SDGs. The course will be guided by the digital sustainability framework (digital maturity, governance, orientation and partnership) and will use real use-cases to develop solutions by students.","overview":"","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":""},{"group":"group2","code":"INFOSYS 707","title":"专题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20707","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 707 - Special Topic","prescription":"","overview":"This course is designed for students keen to explore emerging or niche areas. Learning outcomes include advanced knowledge in a specialised subject area, critically evaluating current research and practice, and communicating ideas in written reports and/or oral presentations.","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程专为热衷于探索新兴或利基领域的学生而设计。学习成果包括专业学科领域的先进知识、批判性地评估当前的研究和实践，以及在书面报告和/或口头演示中交流想法。"},{"group":"group2","code":"INFOSYS 708","title":"专题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20708","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 708 - Special Topic","prescription":"","overview":"This course is designed for students keen to explore emerging or niche areas. Learning outcomes include advanced knowledge in a specialised subject area, critically evaluating current research and practice, and communicating ideas in written reports and/or oral presentations.","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程专为热衷于探索新兴或利基领域的学生而设计。学习成果包括专业学科领域的先进知识、批判性地评估当前的研究和实践，以及在书面报告和/或口头演示中交流想法。"},{"group":"group2","code":"INFOSYS 709","title":"信息系统实践中的当代问题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20709","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 709 - Contemporary Issues in Information Systems Practice - Level 9","prescription":"An advanced study and substantial review of information systems research relating to contemporary issues, providing students with in-depth knowledge of key topics.","overview":"","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":""},{"group":"group2","code":"INFOSYS 720","title":"信息系统研究","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20720","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 720 - Information Systems Research - Level 9","prescription":"A substantive review of research in the discipline of information systems with a focus on behavioural and design science aspects. Behavioural, strategic and social issues relating to the design, implementation, use and impact of information technology applications will be studied.","overview":"This course focuses on information systems (IS) research and the nature of the discipline. A substantive review of research in the discipline of information systems with a focus on two approaches : design science and behavioural Science. Behavioural, strategic and social issues relating to the design, implementation, use and impact of information technology applications will be studied. Since this course is taught at the postgraduate level, it is organised in a multi-modal fashion with lectures, seminars and labs.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard [15] point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect [3] hours of lectures, 7 hours of reading and work on assignments.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"BCom(Hons) - Bachelor of Commerce (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程重点关注信息系统（IS）研究和学科性质。对信息系统学科研究的实质性回顾，重点关注两种方法：设计科学和行为科学。将研究与信息技术应用的设计、实施、使用和影响有关的行为、战略和社会问题。由于本课程是在研究生阶段教授的，因此它以讲座、研讨会和实验室等多模式方式组织。"},{"group":"group2","code":"INFOSYS 750","title":"信息系统中的定量方法","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20750","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 750 - Quantitative Methods in Information Systems","prescription":"A comprehensive review of the methodological issues in information systems research, including detailed coverage of univariate and multivariate data analysis.","overview":"INFOSYS 750 is an introduction to a particular set of quantitative research methods applicable to students intending to pursue research in information systems, operations management, and/or supply chain management. The course is one of a two-part sequence on research methodology (the other being INFOSYS 751). Specifically, this course focuses on applying appropriate quantitative research methods and selecting suitable statistical models and tests to address empirical research questions in management research and the information systems discipline. The statistical package used in this course is R.","topics":"","workload":"This is a standard 15 points course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week. For this course, you can expect [3] hours of lectures, [2] hours of labs/tutorials, [5] hours of readings, assignments, self-study, and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"BCom(Hons) - Bachelor of Commerce (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"INFOSYS 750 介绍了一组特定的定量研究方法，适用于打算从事信息系统、运营管理和/或供应链管理研究的学生。该课程是研究方法论的两部分序列之一（另一部分是 INFOSYS 751）。具体来说，本课程侧重于应用适当的定量研究方法并选择合适的统计模型和测试来解决管理研究和信息系统学科中的实证研究问题。本课程使用的统计包是R。"},{"group":"group2","code":"INFOSYS 751","title":"信息系统中的定性方法","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20751","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 751 - Qualitative Methods in Information Systems","prescription":"Focus is on the conduct and evaluation of qualitative research for the information systems discipline. Reviews various qualitative research methods and ways of analysing qualitative data and the challenges of writing up qualitative research work for conferences and peer-reviewed academic journals.","overview":"The purpose of this course is to provide advanced education and training in qualitative research methods for postgraduate students. The course looks at the underlying research philosophies and possible qualitative research designs in business and management. It also explores how a variety of qualitative research methods, including action research, case study research, ethnographic research and grounded theory, can be used in business and management. The course aims to achieve this through lectures, practical exercises, and class discussions of qualitative studies and examples.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect one 2 hour lecture, 1 hour tutorial (TBC), 5 hours of reading and thinking about the content and on average 2 hours of work on assignments.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"BCom(Hons) - Bachelor of Commerce (Honours)","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"本课程的目的是为研究生提供质性研究方法的高级教育和培训。该课程着眼于商业和管理领域的基本研究理念和可能的定性研究设计。它还探讨了如何在商业和管理中使用各种定性研究方法，包括行动研究、案例研究、人种学研究和扎根理论。本课程旨在通过讲座、实践练习以及定性研究和实例的课堂讨论来实现这一目标。"},{"group":"group2","code":"INFOSYS 757","title":"项目管理与外包","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=INFOSYS%20757","officialTitle":"INFOSYS 757 - Project Management and Outsourcing","prescription":"Takes a project manager perspective to analyse, evaluate and discuss approaches for managing complex projects, focusing on projects involving sourcing of IT/digital business services. Considers strategies for balancing competing demands among scope, time, cost and quality, while working with internal organisational stakeholders, third parties and project team members. Examines the outsourcing project lifecycle.","overview":"The course offers an advanced focus on project management, focusing on projects that involve sourcing of digital technologies and IT-enabled business services. It is expected that the student understands mechanical elements such as how to construct GANTT charts, PERT charts, allocate resources, the 80 hour rule, risk management etc. This course is about exploring the softer side of project management assuming the role of the project manager (not the sub-function leader or project administrator). The project manager does not sit and crunch numbers. The project manager receives information and uses that information to make decisions. The course will cover decisions related to managing competing demands; managing project team and facilitating teamwork; selecting and managing suppliers/service providers. Furthermore, you will learn how to (i) make sourcing decisions and engage in outsourcing initiatives, and (ii) manage outsourcing projects to achieve superior performance. This course is delivered as a seminar type, which requires students to read and prepare in advance, to attend all sessions and to actively participate in group and individual work. Please DO NOT enrol if you are not able to commit to this type of learning. (This is a new course, that integrates materials from two courses that were offered in the past – OPSMGT 757 Project Management and INFOSYS 701 – Global Outsourcing). Therefore, students should expect about half of the materials to focus on core Project Management topics, and another half to emphasise the Outsourcing angle).","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 36 hours of lectures and tutorials, 54 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 60 hours of work on assignments.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"该课程重点关注项目管理，重点关注涉及数字技术和 IT 支持的商业服务采购的项目。希望学生了解机械要素，例如如何构建甘特图、PERT 图、分配资源、80 小时规则、风险管理等。本课程旨在探索项目管理的软性方面，并承担项目经理（而不是子职能领导者或项目管理员）的角色。项目经理不会坐下来处理数字。项目经理接收信息并使用该信息做出决策。该课程将涵盖与管理竞争需求相关的决策；管理项目团队并促进团队合作；选择和管理供应商/服务提供商。此外，您还将学习如何 (i) 做出采购决策并参与外包计划，以及 (ii) 管理外包项目以实现卓越绩效。本课程以研讨会形式授课，要求学生提前阅读和准备，参加所有课程并积极参与小组和个人工作。如果您无法致力于此类学习，请不要注册。 （这是一门新课程，集成了过去提供的两门课程的材料 - OPSMGT 757 项目管理和 INFOSYS 701 - 全球外包）。因此，学生应该预计大约一半的材料将重点关注核心项目管理主题，另一半则强调外包角度）。"},{"group":"group2","code":"OPSMGT 700","title":"医疗分析与运营","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=OPSMGT%20700","officialTitle":"OPSMGT 700 - Healthcare Analytics and Operations","prescription":"Addresses techniques for data-driven decision-making in healthcare. Issues faced when managing healthcare operations will be discussed, with particular reference to the New Zealand context. Mathematical and computer-based techniques for managing operations under uncertainty will be introduced, with a focus on how they can be applied in practice in a healthcare setting.","overview":"Healthcare systems are inherently complex, with many uncertainties and operational challenges. This course introduces techniques for data-driven decision-making in operations. Whilst the particular focus of this course is healthcare, many of the techniques and concepts covered can also be applied in other fields. Students will be introduced to a selection of mathematical modelling and computer-based techniques to solve problems in healthcare operations. As the implementation of these techniques typically involves the use of data, relevant analytics tools will also be introduced. The course will address issues such as model and tool selection, justification of modelling assumptions, and the use of modelling results in decision-making. This course will draw on both the academic literature and case studies.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 2 hours of lectures, a 2 hour lab, 3 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 3 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BCom(Hons) - Bachelor of Commerce (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"医疗保健系统本质上是复杂的，存在许多不确定性和运营挑战。本课程介绍运营中数据驱动决策的技术。虽然本课程的特别重点是医疗保健，但所涵盖的许多技术和概念也可以应用于其他领域。将向学生介绍一系列数学建模和基于计算机的技术来解决医疗保健运营中的问题。由于这些技术的实施通常涉及数据的使用，因此还将引入相关的分析工具。该课程将解决模型和工具选择、建模假设的合理性以及建模结果在决策中的使用等问题。本课程将借鉴学术文献和案例研究。"},{"group":"group2","code":"OPSMGT 702","title":"OSCM 实践中的当代问题","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=OPSMGT%20702","officialTitle":"OPSMGT 702 - Contemporary Issues in OSCM Practice - Level 9","prescription":"An advanced study and substantial review of operations research and supply chain management relating to contemporary issues, providing students with in-depth knowledge of key topics.","overview":"","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":""},{"group":"group2","code":"OPSMGT 741","title":"系统动力学与复杂建模","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=OPSMGT%20741","officialTitle":"OPSMGT 741 - System Dynamics and Complex Modelling","prescription":"The concepts, theories and modelling tools of system dynamics are used to deal with the dynamic complexities arising from interdependencies and interactions amongst various parts and functions within organisations and societies alike. Qualitative and computer modelling are used to gain insight and to foresee the intended outcomes as well as unintended consequences of policies and strategic decisions. All aspects of organisations including HR, IT, operations, marketing and strategy are considered and their interdependencies explored.","overview":"The course will utilise a variety of teaching and learning approaches including, lecture/discussion, modelling workshops, and strategy laboratories. The main objective is to introduce and to reinforce a holistic approach to understand and manage business and other organisations, in a dynamic manner.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 2 hours of lectures/labs, 4 hours of reading and contemplation about the content and 4 hours of work on assignments and/or test and exam preparation per week.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"MCom - Master of Commerce","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"该课程将利用各种教学方法，包括讲座/讨论、建模研讨会和策略实验室。主要目标是引入并强化整体方法，以动态方式理解和管理业务和其他组织。"},{"group":"group2","code":"OPSMGT 780","title":"可持续转型","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=OPSMGT%20780","officialTitle":"OPSMGT 780 - Sustainable Transformation","prescription":"Sustainable transformation aspires to balance and integrate societal, economic and environmental dimensions. Focuses on the interrelationships and influences between the sustainability dimensions from a systems dynamics perspective where vision and strategies are translated to sustainable processes, actions, and performance. Explores sustainable transformation of individuals and families through to organisations, supply chains and society as a whole.","overview":"The goals of the course are to introduce students to: a) What it means for individuals, families, organisations, supply chains and society to transform in a sustainable way, b) How to architect sustainable transformation, c) Behavioural, procedural and technological responses towards sustainable transformation. The sequencing of the topics is significant and illustrates the importance of dealing with sustainability, transformation, systems thinking, visions, goals, balance, strategy, tactics processes, performance, modelling and responding (behaviourally, procedurally and technologically) in a holistic, coherent and integrated fashion. In engaging with the goals a student will develop inter-disciplinary knowledge. Furthermore, they will apply this knowledge within the real-world contexts of individuals, organisations and supply chains.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 2 hours of lectures, a 2 hour tutorial, 2 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 4 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"BCom(Hons) - Bachelor of Commerce (Honours)","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"该课程的目标是向学生介绍：a）以可持续方式转型对个人、家庭、组织、供应链和社会意味着什么，b）如何构建可持续转型，c）对可持续转型的行为、程序和技术反应。主题的顺序很重要，说明了以整体、连贯和综合的方式处理可持续性、转型、系统思维、愿景、目标、平衡、战略、战术流程、绩效、建模和响应（行为、程序和技术）的重要性。在实现目标的过程中，学生将发展跨学科知识。此外，他们还将在个人、组织和供应链的现实环境中应用这些知识。"},{"group":"group2","code":"SCIENT 701","title":"科学家会计与财务","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=SCIENT%20701","officialTitle":"SCIENT 701 - Accounting and Finance for Scientists","prescription":"Builds upon scientific numeracy in exploring the sources, uses and reporting of accounting and financial information in science-based enterprises; application of capital budgeting and valuation theory to science-relevant situations; and key bases for financially-informed project and enterprise decision-making and the management of economic resources.","overview":"This course is a core component of the Bioscience Enterprise programme but also has a limited number of places for students enrolled in other postgrad programmes. There are no prerequisites however you are expected to have demonstrated a high level of achievement in your core discipline. Sound financial management is a key factor in contributing to the survival and success of a corporation. The aim of this course is to introduce the topic of finance and accounting for scientists and provide you with a broad understanding of important elements of the financial management process. After this course you should be able to better interpret corporate financial and accounting information and apply concepts and tools relevant to making financial management decisions in a science based enterprise.","topics":"","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 10 hours per week involved in each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. For this course, you can expect 3 hours of lectures, 4 hours of reading and thinking about the content and 3 hours of work on assignments and/or test preparation.","locations":"Summer Semester 2026","programme":"PGDipBioEnt - Postgraduate Diploma in Bioscience Enterprise","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":"本课程是生物科学企业计划的核心组成部分，但也为就读其他研究生课程的学生提供数量有限的名额。没有任何先决条件，但您应该在您的核心学科中表现出高水平的成就。健全的财务管理是企业生存和成功的关键因素。本课程的目的是向科学家介绍财务和会计主题，并让您对财务管理流程的重要要素有广泛的了解。完成本课程后，您应该能够更好地解释企业财务和会计信息，并应用与在科学企业中制定财务管理决策相关的概念和工具。"},{"group":"group2","code":"STATS 779","title":"统计学专业技能","points":15,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=STATS%20779","officialTitle":"STATS 779 - Professional Skills for Statisticians","prescription":"Statistical software, data management, data integrity, data transfer, file processing, symbolic manipulation, document design and presentation, oral presentation, professional ethics.","overview":"STATS 779 aims to introduce software tools which are useful in applied statistics and statistical research. Many of these tools use a command line approach rather than a graphical user interface. The emphasis is on software which enhances productivity and improves the quality of statistical work. The skills are commonly used in the workplace, but also will be useful for producing academic work such as a dissertation or journal articles. While there are no explicit prerequisites, the course is designed for students with basic knowledge of statistics and the R programming language. This can be acquired through many routes; successful completion of STATS 20x would be one option. Students without this knowledge prior to the course will need to attain it through extra study.","topics":"Leveraging Excel and R for data analysis and statistical algorithms Use of regular expressions for data pre-processing Software that can 1) make things look nice and 2) ensure your results are reproducible Ethics Referencing Data curation Store, retrieve, manage and manipulate data within a database management system (SQL)","workload":"This course is a standard 15 point course and students are expected to spend 150 hours over the course of the semester for each 15 point course that they are enrolled in. This averages to 10 hours a week including break and exam weeks. For this course, a typical workload during a teaching week includes: 2 hours of lectures 2-hour practical, which may be split into 2 1-hour sessions 2 hours of reviewing the course content 4 hours of work on exercises for practicals, quizzes, and/or test preparation","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MSc - Master of Science","prerequisites":"N/A","overviewZh":"STATS 779 旨在介绍在应用统计学和统计研究中有用的软件工具。其中许多工具使用命令行方法而不是图形用户界面。重点是提高生产力和统计工作质量的软件。这些技能通常在工作场所使用，但也可用于撰写论文或期刊文章等学术工作。虽然没有明确的先决条件，但该课程是为具有统计学和 R 编程语言基础知识的学生而设计的。这可以通过多种途径获得；成功完成 STATS 20x 将是一种选择。在课程开始前不具备这些知识的学生需要通过额外的学习来获得这些知识。"},{"group":"internship","code":"COMPSCI 778","title":"实习","points":60,"officialUrl":"https://study.auckland.ac.nz/ords/r/uoa/catalogue/course?p6_code=COMPSCI%20778","officialTitle":"COMPSCI 778 - Internship - Level 9","prescription":"Enables the development of practical knowledge and hands-on experience through a supervised internship in the IT industry. Students complete a research-informed project, and present both written and oral reports of their findings.","overview":"","topics":"","workload":"","locations":"Summer Semester 2025","programme":"MInfoTech - Master of Information Technology","prerequisites":"No pre-requisites or restrictions.","overviewZh":""}],"failures":[],"translation":{"language":"zh-CN","translatedFields":["overview"],"failures":[]}};
